[[entries]]
name = "compiler_binaries"
description = "cycc + cross-compilers (aarch64 Linux/Mach-O, Windows PE, cyrius-x bytecode)"
category = "cyrius"
---
The Cyrius toolchain ships multiple compiler binaries, each
targeting a different host/output triple. All bootstrap from the
same 29 KB seed (`bootstrap/asm`): seed → cybs → cycc (the
cyrc→cybs + cc5→cycc rename landed v6.0.0; the bridge retired v5.11.66).

== PRIMARY (Linux x86_64 self-host) ==

  `cycc`             — main compiler, ~1.51 MB (1,586,184 B) at 6.6.19
                       (1,470,944 B at v6.6.12; 1,247,608 B at v6.6.1;
                       1,141,784 B at v6.5.9; 1,141,696 B at v6.5.8;
                       1,133,440 B at v6.5.6; 1,124,968 B at the v6.5.0
                       opener; 1,108,368 B at the v6.4.82 closeout;
                       grew from the v6.4.0 / v6.3.45 floor of
                       1,024,552 B via the SIMD x86 arc + the v6.4.10
                       top-level-array-sizing fix).
                       self_compile 923 ms at the 6.6.19 gate
                       (648 / 652 ms at v6.5.10).
                       ⚠ The run-to-run spread on the reference box is
                       WIDER than the release-over-release delta (v6.5.8
                       measured 649 / 670 / 701 ms from the SAME binary),
                       so a single number carries no signal — quote the
                       series, never one figure. And size equality is not
                       evidence a change was inert: at v6.5.6 a Mach-O
                       xlat entry left cycc at EXACTLY 1,133,440 B while
                       872,790 bytes differed under `cmp`.
                       Reads .cyr source on stdin; writes
                       ELF executable on stdout. Self-hosts byte-identical
                       via two-step + three-step verify. `cycc` is the
                       FINAL binary name forever (never a version digit).

== CROSSES ==

  `cycc_aarch64`     — Linux ARM64 + macOS Mach-O ARM64 cross.
                       1,114,920 B in the 6.6.19 slot (685,312 B
                       at v6.5.10; 651,904 B at v6.4.82). Built from
                       `src/main_aarch64.cyr`. Apple Silicon also
                       self-hosts byte-identically via
                       `src/main_aarch64_native.cyr` — tracked as
                       `build/cycc-native-aarch64`, 1,323,400 B in the
                       6.6.19 slot, kept in lockstep with the tree by
                       release-gate step 1b since v6.6.6 (the native fork
                       gained the auto-call-main port at v6.0.68; the
                       aarch64 ESYSXLAT gained stat/rename/symlink at
                       v6.0.68, §D1).
  `cycc_win` (cross) — Windows PE32+ cross. 1,561,088 B in the 6.6.19
                       slot (1,021,440 B at v6.5.10; 984,064 B at
                       v6.4.82). Native
                       self-host via `cycc_win.exe` on
                       Windows 11 since v5.5.10 (byte-identical to
                       Linux cross output). Built from
                       `src/main_win.cyr`. Multi-DLL imports (kernel32
                       + shell32 + dxgi) + compiler-emitted indirect
                       calls (callptr) since v6.0.69-.70.
  `cycc_macho`       — Mach-O x86_64 cross (Intel Mac). The Intel-Mac
                       TOOLCHAIN rotted UNGATED for ~2.5 minors while
                       the compiler itself self-hosted; **ach became a
                       first-class release gate at v6.4.59**, alongside
                       ecb / cass / pi.
  `cycc_macho_arm`   — Mach-O aarch64 cross (built when not
                       producing native via cycc_aarch64 on macOS).
                       Built from `src/main_aarch64_macho.cyr`.
  `cycc_cx`          — cyrius-x bytecode entry (`CYX\\0` magic +
                       opcodes; v5.7.12 path B). 1,019,744 B in the
                       6.6.19 slot (606,104 B at v6.5.10; 572,704 B
                       at v6.4.82).
                       Built from `src/main_cx.cyr`. Validated by
                       the cx gates in `programs/checks/cx.cyr`
                       (self-host + roundtrip) PLUS
                       `tests/gates/toolchain/cx_cli.sh`, which covers the
                       installed CLI's `build --target=cx` /
                       `run *.cyx` path — the internal gates prove
                       `src/main_cx.cyr` self-hosts, NOT that the
                       CLI reaches the same emit. (The old
                       `tests/regression-cx-roundtrip.sh` name is
                       gone; no `tests/regression-*.sh` file
                       exists any more.) 2,254,600 B at 6.7.16
                       (core.cyml `overview` has every fork's size).
                       AGNOS (6.7.16, TT-4 – TT-10, TT-9r, TT-9r2):
                       `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1 cycc < src/main_cx.cyr`
                       builds a `cycc_cx` that compiles on agnos — it
                       reads `--version` / `--syntax-only` off the init
                       stack (no /proc there), maps its arena and the
                       preprocessor's 24 MiB buffer with agnos `mmap`
                       (#27), opens includes with agnos `open` (#7, name
                       + length), reads its `CYRIUS_*` environment (the
                       first 256 entries) and runs the toolchain pin
                       check (6.7.12's D5). Through 6.7.15 every compile
                       died in the preprocessor (a raw Linux mmap #9 is
                       agnos's mkdir). Exercised under the fake agnos
                       kernel (`agnos_peer_fake_kernel.sh` axis 14),
                       never on agnos itself. `src/main.cyr`'s agnos
                       build takes agnos's syscall numbers too (TT-10;
                       its arena is refused by name there — it is no
                       agnos compiler, cycc_cx is).

== THE TYPESCRIPT FRONT END IS IN EVERY COMPILER BUT cycc_cx (6.7.15) ==

Since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's D38, the user, 2026-10-18: "Run the TypeScript
front end, as on x86_64 Linux" — PL-13) `--lex-ts` / `--parse-ts` /
`--emit-js` are read by every compiler: x86_64 and aarch64 Linux (the
`cycc_aarch64` cross and the native fork), both macOS compilers, Windows
`cycc.exe` and its Linux-hosted cross compiler, with x86_64 Linux's exit
codes, diagnostics and JS bytes. `cycc_cx` has none. Through 6.7.14 every
compiler but x86_64 Linux's skipped the three flags like any unknown flag
and compiled the TypeScript as cyrius (`--parse-ts` on `let x: number =
1;` failed with a cyrius parse error; on `var x = 42;` it wrote an ELF to
stdout at rc 0), so `ts_test_runner` failed every file there. The cost is
size: the grown forks are ~262 – 336 KB larger (the platform lane's
measurement: `cycc_aarch64` 1,991,240 → 2,254,016 B, `cycc.exe` 2,606,592
→ 2,904,576 B); `build/cycc-native-aarch64` is 2,755,520 B at the 6.7.15
gate (+404,064 B over 6.7.14, the front end the most of it) against
cycc's 2,542,240 B.
`src/main.cyr`'s own cycc stayed byte-identical: its TS pieces moved into
shared includes at their own token positions (`src/frontend/ts/driver*.cyr`).

== RENAME PIN ==

cc3 → cc5 at v5.0.0 was the last name-change penalty paid.
cc5 → cycc + cyrc → cybs at v6.0.0 is the FINAL rename — cycc
stays cycc forever (no cycc6 at v7.0.0, no cycc7 at v8.0.0;
"cyc" was a pre-cut misprediction — the real name is cycc).
`VERSION` file + `--version` output are the only sources of
truth for which release a binary is.

== STANDALONE TOOLS (per `[release].bins` in cyrius.cyml) ==

  `cyrius`              CLI driver — delegates to cycc + helpers.
  `cyrfmt`              Source formatter (analogous to gofmt).
  `cyrlint`             Static analyzer (warnings + dead-code).
  `cyrdoc`              Doc generator (HTML + markdown emit).
  `cybs`                Bootstrap compiler (seed → cybs → cycc;
                        formerly `cyrc`. The separate `bridge.cyr`
                        intermediate was retired at v5.11.66).
  `ark`                 Package manager (.ark distributables).
  `cyrld`               Linker (cyrius-native static linking).
  `cyrius_api_surface`  Public API snapshot diff (v5.7.33).
  `cyrius-lsp`          Language Server Protocol implementation (runs
                        on macOS too since 6.7.16, TL-11 — both macOS
                        tarballs ship it; not on Windows: cycc_win
                        refuses it).
  `cyriusly`            Toolchain-version management CLI (v5.11.10
                        — native cyrius port of scripts/cyriusly;
                        11 verbs: version / list / ls / which / home /
                        help / use / install / uninstall / update /
                        setup / cmdtools). The macOS tarballs ship the
                        compiled one as `bin/cyriusly` and the Windows
                        tarball `cyriusly.exe` since 6.7.16, whose POSIX
                        verbs refuse by name (6.7.16's D84 —
                        cyriusly_native_port_v51110).
  `ts_test_runner`      TypeScript-frontend test harness (v5.11.11
                        — walks .ts/.tsx fixtures via cycc --parse-ts /
                        --lex-ts). Real-corpus smoke against
                        secureyeoman: 2053/2053 .ts + 435/435 .tsx
                        pass at --mode=parse. Works on every POSIX
                        install since 6.7.15 (the front end above;
                        pi: `0/1 passed` → `1/1 passed`).
  `cyaudit`             Dependency audit + policy (`cyrius vet` /
                        `cyrius deny` route here, not to the compiler).
  `cyrsign`             Release signer / verifier: detached Ed25519
                        over a file (the release SHA256SUMS) with
                        sigil's in-tree ed25519 — `keygen` / `sign` /
                        `verify` (the release-signing hardening item, v6.2.31).
  `cyrsign-efi`         Native UEFI Authenticode PE signer (the
                        sovereign `sbsign`; `cyrius sign-efi`, v6.4.47).
                        A separate binary because sigil's RSA scratch
                        banks are ~13 MB of static data.
  `cyrius-init`         Project scaffolder behind `cyrius init` and
                        `cyrius port` (native since v6.2.40; the bash
                        shims are deleted).
  `cxvm`                cyrius-x bytecode VM — runs `.cyx` (`cyrius run
                        prog.cyx`); source programs/cxvm.cyr.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_cli"
description = "cyrius — top-level CLI driver. Orchestrates cycc + auto-deps + standalone tools across build/test/quality/info verbs."
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius` is the user-facing entry point. It auto-resolves
`[deps.*]` declared in `cyrius.cyml` before any
build/run/test/bench/fuzz/check/soak/smoke command (v5.7.21
added the auto-prepend for fuzz; check folded in by v5.7.50).

== BUILD VERBS ==

  cyrius build [--aarch64] [--target=cx] [--no-deps] [--strict-pin] [--pie] [-D NAME] [<src> [<out>]]
  cyrius run [--aarch64] [-D NAME] <source.cyr> [args...]   — args are the PROGRAM's argv (v6.6.5)
  cyrius run <prog.cyx>                       — via cxvm; arguments REFUSED (cx has no guest argv)
  cyrius test [--aarch64] [-D NAME] [<test.tcyr>...]   — 0..N files; exit 0 = pass
  cyrius bench [<path|dir>]
  cyrius check [--with-deps [--no-redirect]] <src.cyr>...  — syntax-only, 1..N files (`--no-redirect`, 6.7.12: check the file itself, not src/main.cyr in place of an include-free file)
  cyrius self                                 — cycc==cycc byte-identical
  cyrius clean [--dry-run]                    — wipe build/
  cyrius lib sync [--dry-run]                 — copy snapshot lib/*.cyr → ./lib/ (v5.11.58)

`-D NAME` (or `-DNAME`) injects `#define NAME` at the top of the bundled
source (preprocessor-style; useful for feature flags). The
`-D` family was added during the v5.6.x optimization arc; since
v6.6.5 it reaches `run`/`test`/`bench`/`fuzz`/`check` too, in
either spelling and in any position.

ONE EMIT TARGET PER RUN (6.7.12, D20 — TL-10). Any two of `--aarch64`,
`--win`, `--agnos`, `--target=<v>` are refused by name on every verb with
the target block — `error: --win and --aarch64 select two emit targets;
pass one`, rc 1 — and `--aarch64 --target=aarch64-bare-metal-elf` is the
one accepted pair. The aarch64 Linux CLI refuses `--agnos` and
`--target=x86_64-bare-metal-elf` by name and labels its builds `[aarch64]`
(D6 — TL-30): agnos and x86_64 bare-metal images build on an x86_64 Linux
host only (through 6.7.11 it wrote an aarch64 Linux binary at rc 0). Since
6.7.13 (TL-2) the aarch64 Linux CLI refuses `--win` by name too —
`error: --win is not available from the aarch64 Linux CLI: its cycc emits
aarch64 only (build PE images on x86_64 Linux or Windows)`, rc 1, nothing
written (through 6.7.12 it wrote an aarch64 ELF named `.exe` at rc 0). On
that host `--aarch64` and `--target=aarch64-bare-metal-elf` run the HOST's
own cycc: `--aarch64` is a no-op, the bare-metal target builds the aarch64
kernel image, and `run` / `test --aarch64` exec the program directly (they
looked for `cycc_aarch64`, the x86_64-hosted cross compiler, and failed).
Since 6.7.14 (TL-1) both macOS CLIs refuse `--win` by name too — `error:
--win is not available from the macOS CLI: cycc emits Mach-O only (build
PE images on x86_64 Linux or Windows)`, rc 1, nothing written (through
6.7.13 they wrote a Mach-O named `.exe` at rc 0 — check `file` on any
`.exe` a Mac build produced before). The rule: PE images are built on
x86_64 Linux or on Windows only. And since 6.7.14 (TL-2) the aarch64 Linux
CLI reads a `[deps.X] target` arch axis as an aarch64 host: a plain
resolve on pi vendors `target = "aarch64"` / `"arm64"` deps and skips
`"x86_64"` / `"x86"` ones, as `--aarch64` does (through 6.7.13 it
vendored the x86_64 dep at rc 0 — re-run `cyrius deps` there after the
pin bump).

`--pie` (TL-28): `cyrius build --pie` is refused by name from the Windows
CLI (cycc.exe has no PIE mode). On macOS (6.7.13, D43 — TL-3) the x86_64
CLI refuses it by name — `error: --pie is not available from the macOS
x86_64 CLI: its cycc writes a fixed-address Mach-O (no MH_PIE mode)` — and
the arm64 CLI accepts it as already met: every arm64 Mach-O is MH_PIE
(mach_header flags 0x00200084). Neither Mach-O compiler reads
`CYRIUS_PIE`; only the CLI flag is refused. So PIE is a Linux ELF mode,
plus arm64 macOS by construction.

A KILLED RUN (6.7.12 TL-24; 6.7.13 D39 — TL-18): a `cyrius` run stopped
by SIGTERM / SIGINT / SIGHUP stops its children and removes its scratch and
temp dir, as in 6.7.12 — then DIES BY THE SIGNAL: the handler resets it to
SIG_DFL, re-raises it and unblocks it. A parent sees WIFSIGNALED / WTERMSIG
N (6.7.11's status); a shell's `$?` is still 128+N. So a Ctrl-C'd `cyrius`
stops a non-interactive shell loop again, and `exec_capture_status` gives
st[1] = 1. (6.7.12 alone exited 128+N — WIFEXITED.) An inherited SIG_IGN
(nohup, a `&` job's SIGINT) is kept. Windows catches nothing. Since 6.7.13
the handler also covers `cyrius capacity` and `_dep_wt_run`'s git queries
and their captures (TL-6), the distlib verify's `dist/.dlverify-<pid>/`
mirror (TL-7), and a child forked just before the signal (TL-8,
`_cbt_fork_tracked`). Since 6.7.14 the handler also covers `cyrius
deps`'s own git (`_git_run`, the clone fork; TL-6) — FORWARDED the signal
and waited for, so git's own cleanup runs (its `index.lock`) and a killed
clone removes its partial cache directory — and their captures, and the
scratch resolve of `deps --locked` / the pin-move dry pass (TL-7: each
temp is recorded BEFORE it is created). `$?` and the signal death (D39)
are unchanged.
THE 6.7.14 CAVEATS ARE CLOSED (6.7.15). (1) A BOUNDED WAIT (6.7.15's D49,
the user, 2026-10-18: keep forwarding the signal to git and waiting, with
a deadline — IT-11): git is sent the signal and given 2 s to exit on it
(tracking mode fwd 2, `_cbt_sig_grace`: a `waitpid(WNOHANG)` poll every
10 ms through nanosleep — `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` on Darwin, both
async-signal-safe), then SIGKILLed and reaped. Real git exits on the
signal, so its own cleanup still runs (`index.lock`, a killed clone's
partial cache directory); a git wrapper first on PATH that traps or
ignores SIGTERM / SIGINT / SIGHUP has 2 s for its cleanup. Measured with a
git that traps TERM and sleeps 8 s: 7,751 → 2,014 ms on x86_64 Linux
(pi 2,037, ecb 2,368, ach 2,480), rc 143, git gone. Through 6.7.14 the
wait had no bound, and a second Ctrl-C was held with it (HUP / INT / TERM
sit in the handler's sa_mask). The CLI's own forked copies (distlib's
per-target children) are still waited for without a bound (fwd 1): their
handler always exits. (2) WHAT IS RECORDED IS WHAT WAS CREATED (IT-12): a
scratch resolve holds HUP / INT / TERM (`_cbt_sig_hold`) from before the
exclusive create (`_aw_open`) until the temp it created is recorded, then
releases them (`_cbt_sig_release`) — so a file another process put at a
temp name the copy SKIPPED survives the kill, with the directory holding
it; a signal arriving during the one create is delivered right after it.
Through 6.7.14 the copy recorded the name it PREDICTED (the removed
`_dep_scr_next_tmp`), and a kill unlinked the planted file (19 of 20
planted files left → 20 of 20). (3) WHAT IS FORGOTTEN IS WHAT WAS RELEASED
(6.7.16, TL-6): a copy's temp is cleared from the record once `_aw_commit`
renames it into place or `_aw_abort` removes it (`_dep_aw_finish`), with HUP
/ INT / TERM held across the commit and the clear — so a killed scratch
resolve never unlinks a file another same-user process put at a temp name a
copy used and released (the filed run: 624 → 640 of 640 planted files left);
a signal during a copy's commit (fsync, close, rename) is delivered right
after it. Windows has no fork or POSIX signals: unchanged. The handler: field_notes/
compiler/gotchas.cyml tools_lane_signals_pairs_paths_and_the_lock_v6712 #1,
tools_lane_host_compilers_masks_and_dry_passes_v6713 #4, #5,
tools_lane_forwarded_children_predicted_temps_and_inherited_ignores_v6714
#4, #5 (the history), and the 6.7.15 infra / tools lane's gotchas entry
("forward the signal" is a contract only with a process that promises
to exit on it; record what you made, not what you expect to make).

`cyrius self` and `cyrius soak`'s self-host steps (TL-3) run WITHOUT
`CYRIUS_POISON` / `CYRIUS_BOUNDS` / `CYRIUS_COVERAGE` /
`CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE`, so they verify the DEFAULT compiler (as `pulsar`,
the cx builds and `cyriusly setup` build it); soak's test leg — the
user's own tests and `.scyr` harnesses — keeps them.

THE ARGUMENT RULE (v6.6.5, every verb and every delegated tool —
cyrlint, cyrfmt, cyrdoc, cyaudit, cyrius_api_surface, ark, cyrsign,
cyrsign-efi, cyrld, cyriusly, cyrius-init, ts_test_runner):
  * flags may appear in ANY position (`lint f --strict` == `lint --strict f`);
  * a `-`-prefixed token the verb does not declare is an ERROR that
    names it — never a file name, never ignored;
  * operand counts are enforced — an extra operand is processed or
    rejected, never silently dropped (`lint a b c` lints all three);
  * a global `-q`/`-v` never shifts the operands;
  * `run` alone is stop-at-first-positional: after the source,
    everything belongs to the program (`run -- p.cyr a` is the same);
  * a value flag given TWICE is an error that names it — except `-D`
    and `--features`, which accumulate (`--features a --features b` ==
    `--features a,b`); before 6.6.5 the last one silently won;
  * `cyrlint --exit-with-count` returns max(count, strict verdict 2),
    so it can no longer switch `--strict`/`--strict-deferrals` off;
  * `cyrius <verb> --help` prints exactly what the parser accepts —
    both read the same spec tables in `cbt/cli_args.cyr`.
Before 6.6.5 each verb hand-rolled its own loop; `clean --dryrun`
deleted build/, `fmt f --chekc` rewrote the file, `build s --strict`
wrote the binary to a file named `--strict`, all at exit 0. Code that
passed an unsupported flag to a verb and relied on it being ignored now
gets a named error.

INCLUDE RESOLUTION IS ENTRY-FILE-RELATIVE (v6.5.7). `cyrius build`
used to resolve every `include` against the process CWD, so a source
in a subfolder could not include a file sitting next to it. The entry
file's directory now rides IN-BAND as `#@incdir <dir>` on line 1 of
the bundled source — `#` opens a comment in cyrius, so older cycc,
cybs and the cx/JS forks skip it untaught. Both out-of-band channels
were dead here: `chdir` breaks the root-relative dep prepend, and an
environment variable was not readable by every compiler when the
marker landed. (This line said "`_read_env` is stubbed on Windows and
cx" until 6.7.16 — stale: Windows reads it through kernel32 since 6.6.6,
the cx compiler since 6.6.10, the agnos `cycc_cx` since 6.7.16, TT-9.
The in-band marker stands for its own reasons.)
Resolution is **CWD-first** — the retry sits after every existing step
in `READFILE`, so no include that resolves today can change meaning; it
can only turn an error into a success.
⛔ The marker is IN-BAND, so a hostile `.cyr` can write one. Honouring
an absolute directory would rebuild the read-anything primitive the absolute-include path bug
removed. Two rules close it: the directory must be RELATIVE and
`..`-free, and it is read ONLY at byte 0 (the CLI writes it ahead of
the dep prepend, so a marker further down stays a comment). The cost is
stated plainly: `cyrius build /abs/dir/x.cyr` gets no marker and keeps
today's behaviour — no reach was bought with a hole.

Also v6.5.7: `cyrius build` proves the output is WRITABLE before
forking. A missing output *directory* used to print a bare `FAIL` with
no `error:` line, because the child had already dup2'd its stdout away
before opening the output, leaving `sys_exit(1)` as its only channel.
And `cyrius capacity <dir>` is refused — `file_exists` returns 1 for a
directory (open-then-close), so `cyrius capacity benches` printed a
full, plausible stats block measuring the auto-prepended stdlib and
exited 0.

CHILD PROCESSES GET THE WHOLE ENVIRONMENT (6.6.11). Every POSIX child
the CLI starts (cycc, test binaries, git, the hasher, `/bin/sh`, ssh)
gets `_envp`, built by `load_environ`. On Linux it read
/proc/self/environ into a fixed 8,192-byte buffer, so every variable
past 8 KB was silently absent in the child; on macOS there is no /proc,
so children ran with an EMPTY environment — no PATH, HOME, TMPDIR,
SSH_AUTH_SOCK or `CYRIUS_*`. Linux now reads to EOF and macOS hands on
the kernel's own entry-stack envp (`_macho_envp()`), uncapped.
Scripts that set `CYRIUS_*` knobs around `cyrius build/test/run` on a
Mac now actually reach the compiler and the test.

A STD FD CLOSED (6.7.16, LF-6). Every child the CLI forks gets the fds its
redirects name when `cyrius` was started with fd 0, 1 or 2 closed (cron, a
daemon, `<&-`): a redirect closes its source only when it is not the target,
as lib/process.cyr's `_proc_redirect` (stdlib_modules `file_locking`). The
verbs covered: `build` (native, `--target=js`, `--target=cx`), `run` (with
run_cx and the cxvm JIT build), `deps` / `update` (the git captures),
`capacity`, `pulsar` and the Mach-O codesign step — and the check driver and
the programs/ tools likewise. Through 6.7.15, with stdin closed, `cyrius build`
said OK for an EMPTY program (exit 0 on x86_64, aarch64 Linux and Intel macOS,
1 on arm64 macOS) and `cyrius deps` refused a clean git cache as tampered;
with stdout closed `cyrius build --target=js` failed with errno 9. And on
Windows (PP-3) a missing or unwritable %TEMP% stops the CLI at once, naming it
by its errno.

PINNED-VERSION RE-EXEC ON WINDOWS (6.6.11). A project whose `cyrius.cyml`
pins another installed version is run by that version's CLI; on Windows
that redirect could not work at all (it looked for `bin/cyrius`, not
`cyrius.exe`, then called a `sys_execve` that is a -1 stub on PE), and
`CYRIUS_RESOLVED=1`, the documented escape from a pin, was ignored.
`cyrius.exe` now honours the flag and runs the pinned `cyrius.exe` as a
child (with `CYRIUS_RESOLVED=1` set, so it does not redirect again),
exiting with its code.

`--strict-pin` (v5.11.58) augments the cycc child's envp with
`CYRIUS_STRICT_PIN=1`, which v5.11.57's `_check_cyml_pin_drift`
in lex.cyr reads to upgrade the pin-drift warning to a hard
exit (code 1). CI gating path for pin-faithful builds — fail
loudly when cwd's `cyrius.cyml [package].cyrius` pin doesn't
match cycc's compile-time version. Since 6.7.12 (D5 — TL-28) the
drift check runs in EVERY host's compiler: cycc.exe and both macOS
compilers warn on a drifted pin and fail under `--strict-pin` /
`CYRIUS_STRICT_PIN=1` (through 6.7.11 it was `#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX` and silent there).

`lib sync` (v5.11.58) copies `~/.cyrius/versions/<X>/lib/*.cyr`
into `./lib/`, where `<X>` = cyml pin or wrapper version.
Third remediation for the shadow-lib warning (besides "delete
./lib/" and `CYRIUS_NO_WARN_SHADOW_LIB=1`).
**v6.4.77: `lib sync` now REFUSES to run inside the cyrius source
repo** and points at the correct refold command. It is right for a
consumer (whose `./lib` is a resolved-dep cache) and destructive in
the cyrius repo, where `./lib` IS the canonical source of every folded
stdlib — a sync there would silently revert every fold.

`cyrius audit` (SCOPED v6.4.78) no longer fmt/lint-walks a consumer's
VENDORED `lib/`. It is gated on `_dep_is_cyrius_source_repo()`, prints
the `scope:` it chose, and NAMES the files that failed (the failing-file
list was collected and then never printed). Note: `audit` still reports
FAIL inside the cyrius repo itself — cycc's own `src/` is not
cyrfmt-clean by design. That is expected, not a regression.

`--target=cx` (v6.4.17–.22) routes `cyrius build` to the cyrius-x
bytecode compiler (cycc_cx), emitting a versioned `.cyx` portable-
bytecode file instead of a native binary. `cyrius run` then executes
it via the installed `cxvm` interpreter — a `.cyx` arg runs directly,
a `.cyr` source compiles-then-runs. The same `.cyx` runs unchanged
on all four hosts: a guest names its syscalls by x86_64 Linux's
numbers (the `.cyx` ABI), and cxvm — not the compiler — serves each
call it lists (programs/cxvm.cyr's op 0x70 is the authority).
⛔ This paragraph said "the compiler renumbers guest syscalls per-OS,
so cxvm needs no translation table" until 6.7.15. No compiler renumbers
a GUEST's call, and believing one did hid a bug (CHANGELOG [6.7.15]
PL-11): cxvm passed a listed call to the host as `syscall(<the guest's
number>, …)`, a RUNTIME number, which the aarch64 and Mach-O compilers
renumber as if it were their own stdlib's — on aarch64 Linux a guest
`lseek` ran getxattr, `kill` ran lseek and `fork` ran close (the parent
took the child branch); on arm64 macOS several calls died of SIGSYS.
Since 6.7.15 each listed call goes through the HOST's own stdlib
wrapper (`_cx_host_sys` / `_cx_host_posix` / `_cx_host_linux`), so it
runs the call it names on every host; open / `F_SETFL` / `F_GETFL`
flags cross field by field (the access mode and eight O_* names; any
other bit dropped off x86_64 Linux and Windows); and the path calls
mkdir / rmdir / unlink / rename / symlink / readlink / stat run on the
host with their pointers translated (PL-12; -38 through 6.7.14, so
`file_write_atomic` failed on cx). Where the host has no route the
answer is -38 (6.7.14's D55): socket / listen / unshare /
timerfd_create / epoll_create1 off Linux, the PE-stub calls (getppid,
kill, dup, dup2, fork, fcntl, …) and stat / readlink on Windows.
Since 6.7.16 the answers are x86_64 Linux's on every host (the `.cyx`
ABI; PL-4 – PL-7): on a macOS host a failed call answers Linux's errno
(ELOOP -40, ENOTEMPTY -39, ENOSYS -38; it was Darwin's, ELOOP -62) and
`kill` sends the signal the guest names (SIGUSR1 delivered Darwin's
SIGBUS); `F_GETFL` carries O_LARGEFILE 0x8000 on every host (6.7.16's
D29); getdents64, mknodat, wait4, a guest's `truncate` (PP-4) and
`poll(NULL, 0, ms)` (a sleep — `sleep_ms` sleeps) run on every POSIX
host, where they answered -38. Still -38: lstat, chmod, a poll with
pollfds. Off x86_64 Linux a cx `stat` fills only mode / nlink / uid /
gid / size / mtime (+ nsec) — cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_v6612.
Gates: tests/gates/codegen/cx_host_dispatch.sh (one fixture: 77 on
every POSIX host's cxvm, 78 on Windows), cx_crossos_rows_run.sh.

`--target=js` (v6.1.12) routes `cyrius build <in.ts|.tsx> <out.js>` to
the host compiler's `--emit-js`. Since 6.7.15 (PL-13, with 6.7.15's D38;
the CLI half kept by 6.7.16's D50) it runs on EVERY POSIX host — x86_64
and aarch64 Linux, both Macs; the CLI follows the include graph
(`_target_cc_has_js()` names the host forks that carry the front end).
⚠ On arm64 macOS it ran only since 6.7.16 (TT-15): the CLI refused
`cyrius build --target=js x.ts out.js` there, rc 1 ("build --target=js
runs this host's cycc, not the compiler another target selector chose"),
because `_js_cc_is_host()` compared the compiler it would run with the
`cycc` slot while that host's default is `cycc_aarch64`; `_cc_default()`
now names the compiler a run with no selector runs. `--aarch64
--target=js` is refused on every host (6.7.12's D20). 6.6.6 – 6.7.14 refused it by name off
x86_64 Linux (before 6.6.6 the CLI renamed a cyrius ELF over the `.js`
and printed OK). On Windows it is still refused by name — `build
--target=js is not available on this target` / `the compiler here
(built from src/main_win.cyr) has --emit-js, but this CLI has no Windows
spawn that writes its output to a file; run it directly: cycc.exe
--emit-js <file.ts> > <out.js>`. Gates: tests/gates/platform/
ts_flags_every_driver.sh, tests/gates/toolchain/emit_js_per_host_fork.sh
(renamed from emit_js_refused_off_x86_linux.sh).

== PROJECT VERBS ==

  cyrius init <name>                          — scaffold new project
  cyrius package                              — compile the [build] entry to build/<output> (the 6.7.10 help row; a missing entry is `error: no such file: <entry>`, rc 1, since 6.7.12 — TL-6; the .ark format is not implemented)
  cyrius publish                              — push to ark registry
  cyrius port [--language=rust|python] <dir>  — port scaffold (v6.5.34)
  cyrius install <pkg>                        — fetch binary release
  cyrius update [--full]                      — lib sync's refresh at the manifest's pin (+ the named-deps refresh); 6.7.12, D18 — TL-16
  cyrius deps [--lock|--no-lock|--verify|--relock] — manifest-driven dep resolve

== QUALITY VERBS ==

  cyrius fmt [--check|--dry|--verbose|--write] <file.cyr>...   (modes EXCLUSIVE, v6.6.5)
  cyrius lint [--strict] [--strict-deferrals] [--exit-with-count] <file.cyr>...
                                              — one cyrlint run; --exit-with-count = TOTAL, clamped to 255
  cyrius doc [--check] <file.cyr>...
  cyrius vet <source.cyr>                     — include-graph audit
  cyrius deny <source.cyr>                    — project-policy enforcement
  cyrius audit [--internal|--internal=platform-check]  — full sweep: self-host + test + fmt + lint (project-scoped since v6.4.78)
  cyrius distlib [--all|--check] [--modular] [profile]  — produce dist-shape stdlib (per-profile `.deps` sidecar, subsetted v6.4.48)
  cyrius capacity [--check|--json] <src.cyr>  — frame/heap/code measurement
  cyrius api-surface [--update]               — public-API snapshot diff (v5.7.33)
  cyrius sign-efi <pe> <key.der> <cert.der> <out>  — native UEFI Authenticode PE sign (v6.4.47; via cyrsign-efi)

`cyrius distlib <profile>` (v6.4.48) SUBSETS the per-profile `.deps`
sidecar: it emits only the stdlib leaves the profile's OWN bundle
actually references, not the whole fold's chain. Before, a `sha`-only
profile's sidecar carried the entire crypto suite's deps (bayan /
keccak / thread / process …).

`--all` / `--check` (v6.5.8): `--all` regenerates the base bundle AND
every `[lib.<name>]` profile declared in the manifest; `--check` does
the same but regenerates into a SIBLING temp and byte-compares instead
of writing, so a CI job can assert `dist/` is in sync without dirtying
the tree. Naming a profile alongside either is an error. Before .8,
`cyrius distlib --all` fell through the unknown-option path and
"succeeded" while doing the wrong thing — one profile, silently.

⭐ THE `.deps` SIDECAR UNDER-REPORTED, AND THAT SWITCHED OFF A REAL
CHECK (v6.5.10, filed from setu 0.8.2). `dist/<lib>.deps` claims to
list the stdlib leaves a fold requires in scope, but it was built ONLY
by scanning bundled module sources for literal `include "lib/X.cyr"`
lines — so it captured what a module INCLUDES, never what it
REFERENCES through the declaration. setu emitted 8 leaves against a
declared 12. This is not merely a misleading file for a human:
`cyrius deps` validates a CONSUMER's `[deps] stdlib` against the dep's
sidecar, so an under-reporting sidecar makes an under-declaring
consumer build `OK`, silently. Measured both directions — a corrected
12-leaf sidecar hard-errored naming the missing leaves; the raw 8-leaf
one passed the same consumer.
The full-bundle sidecar is now the declared `[deps] stdlib` UNIONED
with the include-scan, so it can never under-report relative to either
source (the scan can legitimately catch a leaf the author forgot to
declare).
⚠ BASE BUNDLE ONLY. A profile is a narrow subset of modules, so its
true dep set is a SUBSET of the declaration; unioning the whole
declaration into a `sha` profile would OVER-report and could fail a
legitimately-narrow consumer against the very check this re-enables.
Profiles keep the pruned inference, and the gate asserts it.

⭐ THE SIDECAR IS THE UNION OVER EVERY TARGET, SO ONLY AN x86_64-LINUX
CLI CAN WRITE OR `--check` IT (6.6.11). The verify rounds used to compile
the unit for the HOST only, so the recorded leaf set was whatever that
host's `#ifdef` arms left undefined, and `distlib --check` drifted
between a Mac or an ARM box and Linux CI (15 of 60 ecosystem bundles
have different undefined sets per target). Each round now compiles for
x86_64 Linux, Windows (`CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1`), x86_64 macOS
(`CYRIUS_MACHO=1`) and aarch64 Linux and macOS (`cycc_aarch64`,
`CYRIUS_MACHO_ARM=1`) and classifies the UNION. Only an x86_64-Linux
CLI has a compiler per target, so on macOS, Windows and aarch64
`distlib` REFUSES by name ("only an x86_64 Linux CLI has a compiler for
each") rather than publishing a host-shaped sidecar; a missing
`cycc_aarch64` is named. A symbol undefined on only SOME targets is
credited to a declarer already in the unit, else the one dispatcher
whose peer declares it, else the one non-peer declarer, else a named
refusal. A verify that does not converge in 6 rounds is a REFUSAL
("did not converge in 6 rounds — sidecar NOT written"); it used to
publish the partial set as compile-verified at rc 0. `-v` prints each
leaf a round adds. Measured over 74 ecosystem repos: sidecars
byte-identical to 6.6.10's, `--all` 488 s vs 437 s.

`cyrius sign-efi <pe> <key.der> <cert.der> <out>` (v6.4.47) dispatches
to the standalone `cyrsign-efi` helper (sigil's RSA bignum + PKCS#7)
to Authenticode-sign a PE32+ EFI binary for UEFI Secure Boot. On POSIX
it replaces the CLI; on Windows `cyrius.exe` runs the `cyrsign-efi.exe`
beside it as a CHILD and exits with its code since 6.7.16 (TT-11 —
through 6.7.15 the in-place `sys_execve` was a PE -1 stub and the verb
always failed, rc 127, "helper not found (Linux-only; …)"). A missing
helper, every target: `error: sign-efi: the cyrsign-efi helper is not
installed at <path> (build it with `cyrius build programs/cyrsign-efi.cyr
build/cyrsign-efi`)`, rc 127 (`.exe` on Windows); an installed POSIX
helper that cannot be exec'd: `sign-efi: could not run <path> (execve
error N)`, rc 127.

`cyrius lint` FAILS WHEN THE COMPILER REFUSES THE FILE (6.6.11). Its
compile pre-pass used to lint anyway unless the error message began
with an entry on a SYNTAX allow-list, so `#derive(accessors)` on an
enum, `#derive` on neither a struct nor an enum, and a duplicate
variable all reported `0 warnings`, rc 0. The logic is inverted: every
compiler error must be in a named CONTEXT set (`_lint_msg_is_context` —
an error that only means "this file is not a whole program": `…
requires include "…"`, an array sized by another file's enum, a struct
from a sibling module as a `.field` target, a field type or a `sizeof`)
or lint FAILS and forwards the diagnostic. A compiler that died after
writing only a warning, or in its own words, is also a refusal naming
what it did (killed / `exited N`) — it used to say "could not tell,
linting anyway". Measured on 2,837 sibling-repo source files: verdicts
identical to 6.6.10.
Since 6.7.15 (ST-18) lint's `--syntax-only` pass accepts a field store
on a call to a fn this file does not define — `gp().n = 5;` and a `for`
step `gp().n += 1`, `gp` in a sibling module — with the one
`undefined function 'gp'` warning, and still parses the value (a grammar
error there is reported); through 6.7.14 both were "cannot assign to a
field of a call result: the result is a temporary", rc 1, a reason true
only of a callee KNOWN to return a struct by value (a by-value callee
this file defines keeps the refusal). The full compile is unchanged.
Since 6.7.16 the two shapes that stayed refused are accepted: the cx
compiler's `--syntax-only` (TL-10: it stops after the parse with its
error status and writes no `.cyx`; having no reachability data it also
accepts a REACHABLE call to an undefined fn under the flag, which the
native compilers refuse) and `gp().n = 5;` with `fn gp(): *Foo` defined
HERE and `Foo` a sibling's struct (ST-9; `: Foo` by value, `: **Foo`
and `: *i64` keep "the result is a temporary"). And with Foo in a
sibling module lint also accepts (GB-5, GB-5r, GB-7, GB-24) a call in an
unknown receiver's chain (`a.m<i64>(1)`, `a.h.k(1)`, `a.h.m<i64>(1)`), a
named argument in such a call (`a.k(x: 1)`), the chain on a single-name
method call's result (`a.r(1).g`, `a.r(2).k(3)`), a local of the
sibling's struct (`var a: Foo;`, `var b: Box<i64>;` — `var p: *Foo;` and a
scalar keep the uninitialised refusal), a sibling's explicit-generic call
`g<i64>(x)` (RV-13) and chains on a sibling file's struct — a syntax
error inside such a call is still reported. And lint REFUSES what the
build refuses (SS-2, SS-6): `Some(5).n = 1;` in a fn above its enum (the
payload constructor read as a sibling's fn) and a store through
parentheses `(s).a = 1;` / `+= 1` / a step (it passed them silently).

== TESTING VERBS ==

  cyrius test [<file|dir>...]                 — no args: [test] files, then every tests/**.tcyr (recursive); a DIR walks recursively (6.7.6)
  cyrius bench [<file|dir>]                   — auto-discovers benches/*.bcyr + tests/*.bcyr
  cyrius fuzz [<file|dir>]                    — auto-discovers fuzz/*.fcyr + tests/*.fcyr
  cyrius soak [N]                             — N-iter self-host loop + tests/scyr/*.scyr
  cyrius smoke                                — auto-discovers tests/smcyr/*.smcyr
  cyrius coverage [--full] [--min <pct>] [<dir>]  — reference-coverage report over the PROJECT's own src/ (v6.4.72)
  cyrius doctest <file.cyr>                   — run `# example:` blocks

`cyrius tests` was REMOVED at 6.7.12 (D21 — TL-7): an unknown command, rc 1,
whose refusal names the replacement — ``error: `cyrius tests` was removed at
6.7.12: use `cyrius test <dir>` (`cyrius test tests` for tests/)`` — before
any parse, resolve or run. It had been a deprecated alias since 6.7.6. The
test verb is `cyrius test <file|dir>...`.

SUBFOLDER CALLOUT + RECURSIVE WALKERS (v6.5.7). `test` / `bench` / `fuzz`
now accept a FILE **or a DIRECTORY** argument, and their walkers recurse
— `cyrius bench benches/perf` walks that subtree. A directory argument
previously ran nothing, printed nothing and exited 0, so a gate written
that way passed over zero benchmarks. A path that does not exist is now
refused rather than silently compiling the auto-prepended stdlib into a
do-nothing program. (`cyrius fuzz` also gained `tests/` discovery at
v6.5.6 — `cyrius init` scaffolds its harness to `tests/<proj>.fcyr` and
creates no `fuzz/` dir, so every scaffolded project answered "No fuzz
harnesses found in fuzz/" against a harness the scaffolder had written
seconds earlier. Third instance of that family after `test` v6.4.72 and
`bench` v6.4.78; all three verbs return 0 when they find nothing, which
is how it stayed invisible.) ⚠ `test` did NOT take a directory from
v6.5.7 to 6.7.5 (`not a file`; the walk was `cyrius tests <dir>`); since
6.7.6 it does, and a bare `cyrius test` finding nothing exits non-zero
(test_scope_and_one_test_verb_v676).

`cyrius coverage` (FIXED v6.4.72) defaults to the project's OWN `src/`,
walked recursively with `lib/` and `dist/` excluded. It used to report on
the VENDORED stdlib, so a downstream project got "Functions: 0/1097" (a
stdlib figure, permanently ~0 for any project) and a "Libraries: 67/68
covered" line that read like good news about the project but described the
stdlib. `--full` restores the old vendored-stdlib behaviour; `--min <pct>`
exits non-zero below the floor (CI gate); the mode is now STATED in the
summary with a `[reference coverage — a floor, not a correctness proof]`
caveat. The test corpus is also walked recursively, so a suite at
`tests/<name>.tcyr` is found (it used to hardcode `tests/tcyr/`).

⛔ v6.5.8 FOUND FOUR MORE DEFECTS IN `coverage`, EVERY ONE OF WHICH
PRINTED A NUMBER AND EXITED 0:
  - a FIXED 1 MiB test corpus that silently truncated (mis-measuring
    THIS repo by 33 functions, 179 → 212). The failure is
    ANTI-CORRELATED with the signal — it degrades as the suite grows,
    so it punished the projects testing most. Now grow-and-retry.
  - an off-by-one that missed a symbol at the corpus end.
  - **`pub fn` was invisible to the tool whose job is counting public
    functions** — it matched only a bare `fn ` at line start, while the
    header generator in the SAME file already handled the v6.5.0
    explicit-public spelling.
  - fail-open on an empty measurement: measuring nothing printed a
    `note:` and returned 0. Measuring nothing is not passing.
  - plus a SUBFOLDER CALLOUT (`cyrius coverage src/sub`): a positional
    was previously dropped on the floor, the scan ran against `./`
    anyway, and the run exited 0. An argument the tool accepts and does
    not act on is worse than one it rejects, because the output looks
    like an answer to the question that was asked.

`coverage` DOES NOT COUNT THE ENTRY POINT `main` (6.6.11). A public fn
counts as covered only when a test NAMES it, and a program's depth-0
`main` is run by its own file's `syscall(60, main())` (or the epilogue's
auto-call), never by a test — so no project with an entry point could
reach 100 % (ganita sat at 139/141 and bayan at 501/503 with `main` the
only misses; now 139/139 and 501/501). It is excluded by the same rule
`cyrius header` uses (`_src_is_entry_fn`, cbt/srcscan.cyr), by exact
name only — `mainx` and `domain` still count.

`cyrius header` WRITES RETURN TYPES (6.7.11, TL-11): `cyr_val` (8 B or
less, a pointer, a scalar, `Str`), `cyr_val2` for a 9–16 B struct or a
`dyn` under `#ifndef _WIN64` (on Win64 a named comment, no prototype —
Win64 C returns anything over 8 B through a hidden pointer), `cyr_valN`
by value over 16 B (the callee writes whole words, so the buffer is the
rounded size), `cyr_val2` for a two-value return under `#if
defined(__x86_64__) && !defined(_WIN64)` (aarch64 returns it in x0:x2,
not AAPCS64's x0:x1). A return it cannot size from the one file
(another file's struct, a generic instance, a struct holding one, a
struct with `#ifdef`'d fields, three values) is refused by name, rc 1,
the rest of the header still written. A variadic tail is `...`, a fully
variadic fn `f(void)` with a comment. Since 6.7.12: a `: Result` /
`: Option` / `: Tagged` (or the file's own `enum E: stack`) return is
`cyr_val2` (tag in w0, payload in w1) under `#if defined(__x86_64__) &&
!defined(_WIN64)`, a named comment on aarch64 (x0:x2) and Win64; a public
fn whose signature says a word (none, or `: i64`) but whose body returns
the pair is refused by name, rc 1 (6.7.12's D23 — TL-22); a SIMD vector
return is refused by name, rc 1 (6.7.12's D24 — TL-23). Since 6.7.13 a
public fn whose signature says a word and whose body applies `?` to a
pair call is refused by name too, through `'<callee>(..)?'` (6.7.12's D23
with 6.7.13's D37 — TL-5), and a SIMD vector PARAMETER is refused by name,
rc 1 — `'<f>' takes '<param>: <type>': a SIMD vector parameter, which no
C11 type carries` (a `*V` parameter stays `cyr_val`; TL-4). lib/result.cyr's
`ok_via` / `err_via` are declared `: Result`, so `cyrius header
lib/result.cyr` is rc 0 again (D37 — TL-16). Since 6.7.14 (TL-3) a
parameter list that wraps lines is read to its own `)`: every parameter
is printed (through 6.7.13 only the FIRST line was, so the prototype
dropped parameters — a C caller compiled against it passed garbage in
them and now meets "too few arguments": regenerate the header), a vector
parameter on a continuation line is refused by name (rc 1), and a list of
blanks alone (`fn e1( )`) prints `(void)`. lib/net.cyr's `sock_send_a` /
`sock_recv_a` are declared `: Result` (TL-4), so `cyrius header
lib/net.cyr` prints both as `cyr_val2` (their callers are
byte-identical). The documented limit stands for user code: a pair
reached through ANOTHER file's fn or a method is not seen, so declare
such a wrapper `: Result`. Every source scanner
(header, coverage, the LSP index, distlib, `[embed]`, api-surface,
cyrdoc, type-audit) sees a `const fn` and balances a generic type's
`<..>` in a parameter (TL-2) — `>>` / `>>>` close two / three levels,
and after a parameter's depth-0 `=` (a default) `<` is the operator
(and since 6.7.12 `cyrius type-audit` counts a generic `fn f<T>(..)`,
stepping the `<..>` between its name and its `(` — TL-8). A `src/` whose only public
fn is `main` has nothing to measure and gets the "no public functions
found … not a pass" error.

⛔ THE FAIL-OPEN VERB FAMILY (v6.5.8). `doc`, `vet`, `deny`, `audit` and
`coverage` all absorbed a bogus argument and exited 0. `doc <missing>`
printed NOTHING — the worst shape, because empty output from a doc
generator is indistinguishable from "this file has no documentation", so
a mistyped path reads as a finding about the code. `vet <missing>`
reported "no dependencies" — a clean bill of health for a file that was
never opened. `audit` swept the whole project anyway, so a mistyped flag
produced a full-looking PASS for something nobody asked for. All five now
validate the target BEFORE delegating and name what they could not read.

== INFO + LSP ==

  cyrius lsp                                  — build + install cyrius-lsp
  cyrius version                              — toolchain version (+ manifest-pin line in projects, v5.11.58)
  cyrius version --project                    — project's VERSION file
  cyrius which                                — path to cycc
  cyrius repl                                 — expression REPL
  cyrius help                                 — full verb list

== --version BEHAVIOR (v5.11.58) ==

When `cyrius --version` runs inside a project tree (cwd has
`cyrius.cyml` with `[package].cyrius = "X.Y.Z"`), the output
appends a second line:

  cyrius 5.11.57
  manifest-pin: 5.11.55 (drift — wrapper is 5.11.57)

The `(drift — wrapper is X.Y.Z)` suffix only appears on
mismatch; clean match prints just `manifest-pin: 5.11.55`. The
drift surface helps consumers spot toolchain skew the moment
they check the wrapper, before the cycc pin-drift warning fires
during a build.

CROSS-REF: see `compiler_env_vars` for `CYRIUS_STRICT_PIN` +
`CYRIUS_NO_WARN_PIN_DRIFT` env knobs (cycc-side, v5.11.57).

[[entries]]
name = "tcyr_test_format"
description = ".tcyr — assertion-driven test format with auto-discovery"
category = "cyrius"
---
`.tcyr` files are runnable Cyrius programs that follow the
test-runner contract. `cyrius test` (no args) auto-discovers
`tests/*.tcyr` RECURSIVELY — so both `tests/tcyr/*.tcyr` (this
repo's layout, 403 files at 6.6.11) and a flat
`tests/<name>.tcyr` (what `cyrius init` scaffolds) are found.
`cyrius test <file|dir>...` takes files AND directories since 6.7.6:
a directory runs every `.tcyr` under it (recursive), several operands
run each file once, and a directory holding no `.tcyr` is a named
failure. `cyrius tests <dir>` was a deprecated alias in 6.7.6 – 6.7.11,
REMOVED at 6.7.12 (D21: an unknown command, rc 1, naming `cyrius test
<dir>`). (Until 6.7.6
the singular verb refused a directory with `not a file` and the walk
was the plural `cyrius tests <dir>`; this entry said "a FILE or a
DIRECTORY" until the 6.6.11 refresh — wrong then, true since 6.7.6.)

⚠ HOW `cyrius test` GRADES A TEST (cycc 6.6.11). Exit 0
is no longer enough. The runner captures the test's stdout (and echoes
it back) and applies the rule every other `.tcyr` reader in cyrius
already used (the check driver, CI, the cross-OS runner): when the
test's SOURCE contains `assert_summary(`, the LAST stdout line that
starts `N passed, M failed` must exist with N >= 1 and M == 0. So each
of these, which scored PASS before, now FAILS by name:
  - a test that died before its summary with exit 0
    ("no assert summary on stdout: the test stopped before
    assert_summary() or ran nothing");
  - a test that asserted nothing (`0 passed, 0 failed`);
  - a body run TWICE — a defined `main` plus a top-level `main();`,
    which the epilogue's auto-call of `main` runs again (measured:
    `1 passed, 1 failed`, exit 0).
A test that never calls `assert_summary(` is graded on its exit code
alone, and a summary line reporting failures fails whatever the exit
code. So end a `.tcyr` with EXACTLY ONE summary:
  - no `main`:   `var r = assert_summary();` (+ an explicit exit), as
                 below;
  - with `main`: `return assert_summary();` inside it, and the file
                 ends `syscall(60, main());` — never a second bare
                 `main();`.

Standard shape:

    include "lib/string.cyr"
    include "lib/syscalls.cyr"
    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    include "lib/assert.cyr"

    alloc_init();

    test_group("widget basics");
    assert_eq(widget_size(), 16, "widget is 16 bytes");
    assert_eq(widget_id(0), 0, "ids start at 0");

    var r = assert_summary();
    syscall(60, r);   # exit 0 = all pass; non-zero = failure count

⚠ TWO REFINEMENTS THE SCAFFOLD TEMPLATE NOW CARRIES (v6.5.6), worth
copying into any new suite:
  1. CLAMP the count. A wait status is only 8 bits, so a suite with
     exactly 256 / 512 / 768 failing assertions truncates to 0 and
     scores PASS. `if (r > 0) { r = 1; }` before exiting.
  2. Exit through `sys_exit_group(r)`, not `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)`.
     `sys_exit` is exit(2) — it ends only the CALLING thread — so a
     suite that has spawned any worker hangs at its own epilogue with
     no diagnostic.

`cyrius test` auto-prepends every dep distfile + the
`[deps].stdlib` list from `cyrius.cyml` so the runner doesn't
need to write include directives for stdlib in scope — and since
6.7.6 the TEST SCOPE too (`[test] stdlib` / `modules`, `scope = "test"`
deps, a directory's `test.cyml`), unless `[build] test_standalone =
true` or `--no-deps` (test_scope_and_one_test_verb_v676).

Output convention:
  `=== <test_group name> ===`
  `  PASS: <assertion msg>`  (only on --verbose; default is silent on pass)
  `  FAIL: <assertion msg> (got X, expected Y)`
  Final line: `N passed, M failed (T total)`

[[entries]]
name = "bcyr_fcyr_scyr_smcyr_formats"
description = ".bcyr (bench) / .fcyr (fuzz) / .scyr (soak) / .smcyr (smoke) — test-adjacent harness formats"
category = "cyrius"
---
Four sibling formats to .tcyr, each scoped to a specific
validation cadence.

== .bcyr — BENCH HARNESS ==

`cyrius bench` auto-discovers `benches/*.bcyr` AND `tests/*.bcyr`,
walking both RECURSIVELY (v6.5.7); a file or directory argument is
honoured. Same source-shape as .tcyr but uses `lib/bench.cyr`'s
timing helpers:

    bench_start("op_name");
    ... work ...
    bench_end("op_name");
    bench_report();

For sub-microsecond ops where `clock_gettime` overhead dominates:

    bench_batch_start("hot_op", 1000000);   # 1M iterations
    var i = 0; while (i < 1000000) { ...; i = i + 1; }
    bench_batch_stop();

`scripts/bench-history.sh` runs the 3-tier suite → `BENCHMARKS.md`
+ appends `bench-history.csv`. Since 6.7.11 (TL-7) BENCHMARKS.md prints
the size rows in BYTES (`2,114,376 B`), the CSV's file-size row is
`size/cycc_bytes` (was `size/cc5_bytes`, every row renamed), and
`sh scripts/bench-history.sh --report` regenerates BENCHMARKS.md from
the CSV without a run. Per CLAUDE.md it is a RELEASE GATE,
not a closeout-only step: EVERY `.NN` cut runs it on a quiet box
before `version-bump.sh`, and the headline delta (self_compile ms +
cycc size) goes in the CHANGELOG entry.

== .fcyr — FUZZ HARNESS ==

`cyrius fuzz` auto-discovers `fuzz/*.fcyr` AND `tests/*.fcyr`
(v6.5.6), walking both RECURSIVELY (v6.5.7); a file or directory
argument is honoured. Each harness exercises a module with stress
patterns. Exit 0 = pass, non-0 = fail, 139 = SIGSEGV crash.
Standard fuzz harnesses ship with stdlib: `hashmap.fcyr`,
`vec.fcyr`, `string.fcyr`, `freelist.fcyr`.

`cyrius fuzz --compiler [N]` runs mutation fuzzing against cycc
itself (separate from project-level harnesses).

== .scyr — SOAK HARNESS ==

`cyrius soak [N]` runs N iterations of the full validation loop
plus walks `tests/scyr/*.scyr`. Long-running validation —
overnight loops. Catches: non-determinism, memory leaks, state
corruption, codegen drift.

  cyrius soak 100                  # quick overnight
  cyrius soak 1000                 # full soak
  cyrius soak 10 "repo1 repo2"     # custom downstream-repo list

Example: `tests/scyr/alloc_pressure.scyr`.

== .smcyr — SMOKE HARNESS ==

`cyrius smoke` auto-discovers `tests/smcyr/*.smcyr`. Quick sanity
checks — meant to run in seconds, gate releases. Example:
`tests/smcyr/compile_minimal.smcyr`.

[[entries]]
name = "cyml_format"
description = "CYML (.cyml) — TOML header + markdown body in one file. Used by vidya, cyrius.cyml, this-very-file."
category = "cyrius"
---
CYML — Cyrius Markup Language — is TOML above the delimiter,
markdown below it. Implemented in `lib/cyml.cyr`.

== THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES ==

Three formats in the AGNOS ecosystem each solved half:
  - TOML: typed fields, but prose in triple-quoted strings is ugly
  - Markdown: agents read it natively, but no type system
  - YAML frontmatter: hybrid that works, but YAML brings known
    bugs (indentation-sensitive, Norway problem, implicit typing)

CYML is the deliberate fourth option: TOML on top, markdown
underneath, `---` separator.

== SHAPES ==

Single-entry:

    [meta]
    name = "example"
    tags = ["tutorial"]

    ---

    Everything below the delimiter is markdown.
    Full prose. Code blocks. Lists. No escaping.

Multi-entry (vidya pattern; this file is the canonical example):

    [topic]
    id = "tooling"

    [[entries]]
    name = "first_entry"
    type = "best_practice"

    ---

    First entry's markdown body.

    [[entries]]
    name = "second_entry"
    type = "gotcha"

    ---

    Second entry's body.

== PARSE RULES ==

  1. File starts in TOML mode.
  2. `[[entries]]` on its own line starts a new entry TOML header.
  3. `---` on its own line switches to markdown body.
  4. Next `[[entries]]` or EOF ends the current body.
  5. TOML above the first `[[entries]]` is file-level metadata.
  6. Leading/trailing whitespace on body is trimmed.
  7. Body is stored as raw string — no markdown parsing on the
     library side; the consumer can run a markdown parser if
     they want HTML output.

== API ==

    var doc = cyml_parse(data, len);
    var n = cyml_doc_entry_count(doc);
    var e = cyml_doc_entry(doc, 0);
    var hdr = cyml_entry_header(e);    # TOML pointer
    var body = cyml_entry_body(e);     # markdown pointer

    # Convenience for single-entry files:
    cyml_split(data, len, &hdr, &hdr_len, &body, &body_len);

Zero-copy parser — returns pointers into the input buffer.
22-assertion test suite.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_cyml_manifest"
description = "cyrius.cyml — project manifest. Top-level package + build + release + deps blocks."
category = "cyrius"
---
Every Cyrius project carries a `cyrius.cyml` at the repo root.
Pure CYML; the file-level header (above any `[[entries]]`) is
the manifest. No `[[entries]]` block is used here — the manifest
is single-entry.

== TOP-LEVEL BLOCKS ==

    [package]
    name        = "myapp"
    version     = "${file:VERSION}"   # interpolation
    description = "..."
    license     = "GPL-3.0-only"
    repository  = "https://github.com/..."

    [build]
    src    = "src/main.cyr"
    output = "build/myapp"

    [release]
    bins        = ["myapp", "myapp-cli"]
    cross_bins  = ["myapp_aarch64"]
    scripts     = ["scripts/install.sh"]

    cyrius = "5.8.27"     # MINIMUM compiler version (v5.6.x+)

    [deps]
    stdlib = ["syscalls", "string", "alloc", "str", ...]

    [deps.<name>]
    git     = "https://github.com/MacCracken/<name>.git"
    tag     = "X.Y.Z"
    modules = ["dist/<name>.cyr"]

== INTERPOLATION ==

  ${file:PATH}       — read PATH (relative to manifest), trim
                       trailing whitespace. Used for VERSION.
  ${env:NAME}        — environment variable.
  ${file:foo:default} — file with default if missing.

== `cyrius` MINIMUM-VERSION CHECK ==

`cyrius build` reads the top-level `cyrius` field and refuses to
compile if the installed toolchain is older. Same pattern as
Rust's `rust-version`, Go's `go` directive, Zig's
`minimum_zig_version`. Bump when:

  - Using a new language feature (slices, multi-width types, etc.)
  - Using a new stdlib module that wasn't in earlier releases
  - Using a new compiler directive
  - Hitting a bug fix in a specific patch release

== `[release]` LISTS ==

`bins` / `cross_bins` / `scripts` are the SINGLE source of truth
for what the release pipeline ships. `release.yml`, `install.sh`,
and `cbt/pulsar.cyr` all read from this manifest — no list is
duplicated across multiple files. Adding a new binary requires
exactly one edit (here).

== `[lsp] skip` (6.7.16, TL-13) ==

    [lsp]
    skip = ["bootstrap/cybs.cyr"]   # paths from the project root

The files cyrius-lsp never compiles (it publishes an empty list for them —
lsp_and_plugins). A declared key: a vocabulary row (`cyrius help
manifest`), a `--print-config` row `lsp.skip`; an unknown `[lsp]` key is
warned by name, and a value that is not a path or an array of paths is
refused by name. An installed 6.7.15 CLI warns `cyrius.cyml [lsp] is not a
known section and nothing reads it` (harmless). cyrius's own manifest skips
bootstrap/cybs.cyr and src/main_aarch64_native.cyr (TL-15r2).

== `[deps]` AUTO-PREPEND ==

`stdlib = [...]` lists module bare-names (`syscalls`, not
`lib/syscalls.cyr`); `cyrius` auto-prepends each as
`include "lib/<name>.cyr"` to bundled source for
build/test/bench/fuzz/check/soak/smoke. Avoids repeating include
lines in every test/bench file. Test-only leaves (`assert`, `bench`)
belong in `[test] stdlib` since 6.7.6, out of the production prepend
(test_scope_and_one_test_verb_v676).

⚠ A stdlib leaf and a named dep's THIN PROFILE of the same package
(6.6.11). When a stdlib leaf `<leaf>` (say `sigil`) is in the
resolution AND a `[deps.<leaf>]` git dep — yours or a dependency's —
names that package's thin profile (`dist/sigil-mldsa.cyr`, or a
`<leaf>_<x>.cyr`), the profile is neither vendored nor auto-included:
the stdlib fold already carries it, and both copies used to land in one
unit (bote over libro: 232 duplicate fns, five arity errors, a failed
build). A stale vendored copy is removed, with one `note:` each. Since
6.7.13 (TL-13) the kept fold counts as PULLED for the named dep, so
`cyrius distlib` records neither the fold nor what it includes in the
bundle's sidecar; a sidecar in that shape shrinks at its next regen.

`[deps.<name>]` blocks declare git-resolved deps. Materialized
into `lib/<name>_<basename>.cyr` (namespaced). Lockfile at
`cyrius.lock` records (1) a `commit\t<sha>\t<name>\t<git>\t<tag>`
line per git dep (the release-integrity hardening item, v6.2.30 — a repointed tag is refused
against it), (2) a `<sha256>  lib/<file>` line per vendored file,
sorted, and (3) since v6.6.4 a `cyrius\t<pin>` TRAILER naming the
stdlib pin the hashes were taken under. The trailer is what lets
the resolver tell "the pin changed" (re-lock silently) from "the
pinned snapshot's bytes changed under the same pin" — the latter is
REFUSED by name with both hashes (hisab 2026-09-13: a `cyrius build`
under an unchanged pin rewrote a committed lib file and re-locked
it, and `deps --verify` passed on the mutation). `cyrius deps
--relock` is the explicit accept. A lock written before 6.6.4 has
no trailer: it fails open for ONE resolve and comes back stamped.

6.7.12 — THE LOCK, THREE MORE FACTS. (1) The SHA-256 is computed by the
CLI in process (TL-17; digests identical to sha256sum) — no hasher on
PATH is needed. (2) `deps --verify` / `--locked` NAME a CRLF checkout of
the locked bytes and give `lib/** -text` in `.gitattributes` as the
remedy; a CRLF checkout still FAILS (exact bytes are the proof — never
re-lock the converted bytes); `cyrius init` writes, and `cyrius port`
creates or appends, that `.gitattributes` line (D10 — TL-27). (3) A
successful resolve prints a `note:` naming each lib/ file outside the
declared footprint, and every resolve and `--locked` print a `note:` for
a kept commit pin of a dep's OLD tag (D25: the row stays, fail-closed;
delete cyrius.lock to re-pin) (TL-11, TL-13). Since 6.7.13 (TL-9) a plain
`cyrius deps` drops a lock row whose file is gone from lib/ and is not
vendored, and re-writes the lock once (6.7.12 needed `rm <file> && cyrius
deps --lock`); `deps --verify` names such a row `FAIL: lib/<f>.cyr
(missing from lib/)`. The trailer is ONE pin for every row: at a moved
pin `cyrius deps` / `cyrius build` refuse to re-lock rows they did not
re-vendor (6.7.12's D19 — lib_sync_relock_v6617), and since 6.7.13 so do
`deps --lock` and a scoped `lib sync` (D42); a refused resolve writes
nothing (D41).

Every `[deps.NAME]` field that becomes a component of that path is
validated before any mkdir, clone or cache check: the header NAME
since 6.6.13 (the deps-header dot-dot path bug) and the `tag` since 6.6.16 (the deps-tag dot-dot path bug) refuse `..`,
a leading `/` or `-`, a `.`-led component, `\`, control bytes and an
empty value, naming the refused value with non-printing bytes escaped
(a tag of `../../x` used to make git create directories outside the
cache before it rejected the ref). Still unvalidated (backlog): the
read side — `modules` entries and a transitive `path`.

Since 6.6.5 (the dep-cache tamper-check hardening item) the cached checkout in
`$CYRIUS_HOME/deps/<name>/<tag>` is VERIFIED on every resolve, not
just pinned: `.git` must be a real directory, `HEAD` must equal
`refs/tags/<tag>^{commit}`, `git fsck` must pass, and the working
tree must hash back to HEAD's tree with no extra files — computed
in a PRIVATE throwaway index, so the cache's own stat cache,
`assume-unchanged`/`skip-worktree` bits and config take no part and
nothing inside the shared cache is written. Untagged git deps get
the content half too (they float on the pin, not on the bytes).
What this means in practice:

  * a `touch` / `cp -a` / uid-mapped-namespace `git status` on the
    cache is FINE (through 6.6.4 each of those was refused as
    "tampered", for every project on the box);
  * an edit inside the cache is REFUSED even when its mtime is
    restored, even under `assume-unchanged`, and even if the cache
    holds a local commit — as is any file the tag does not carry,
    including one hidden by `.gitignore`;
  * a hand-staged cache directory with no `.git` is REFUSED rather
    than silently trusted, as is a clone of a DIFFERENT repository
    parked at that name and tag (`remote.origin.url` must be the url
    the manifest declares — compared with one trailing `/` and one
    trailing `.git` normalised away, because `…/x` and `…/x.git` are
    the same repository and comparing them exactly refused 19 live
    declarations). `path = "../sibling"` beside `git` / `tag` remains
    the sanctioned local route — since 6.7.6 read only in LOCAL mode
    (`CYRIUS_LOCAL=1` / `--local`; cyrius_cyml_git_first_local_switch_v676);
  * the tree is compared as git compares it AND as RAW BYTES
    (`hash-object --no-filters`), because a `.gitattributes` carried
    BY THE TAG (`* text=auto`) otherwise launders a line-ending-only
    edit past `diff-files`. A raw difference is accepted only when
    re-running the tag's blob through the same conversion
    (`cat-file --filters`) reproduces the working tree exactly, so a
    legitimately CRLF checkout (`core.autocrlf=true`) still resolves;
  * on native Windows NONE of this runs — `sys_fork` does not exist
    there, so no git command can — and a pre-populated cache resolves
    with a warning saying it was not verified, rather than a refusal
    with a reason that would be untrue;
  * the cache's own git config gets no vote: what a command-line
    `-c` can override is overridden, and what it cannot is REFUSED —
    `filter.*` / `diff.*` drivers (a program git RUNS while hashing,
    which can print whatever bytes it likes), `include.*` /
    `includeIf.*` (git follows them; `config --local --list` does not
    print what they pull in), `core.autocrlf` / `core.eol`,
    `core.worktree`, `extensions.worktreeConfig`, and any
    `.git/info/attributes`;
  * the refusal names the cache path and an OFFLINE recovery:
        rm -f <cache>/.git/index
        git --no-replace-objects -C <cache> -c core.fsmonitor=false \
            -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null reset -q --hard refs/tags/<tag>
        git -C <cache> clean -qffdx
    Four reasons have no local repair and say so: an unreadable
    `.git`, a damaged object store, hostile configuration inside
    `.git`, and a cache of the wrong repository. For those, delete
    the cache directory and re-resolve. The damaged-object-store
    refusal quotes git's own first line, so a malformed OLD commit
    does not read like a forged object.

⚠ What it proves is narrower than "the checkout IS the tag":
everything it reads lives inside the cache, so a local tamper commit
with `git tag -f` moved onto it is indistinguishable offline. The
`cyrius.lock` commit pin is the bound, and it is trust-on-first-use.
Keep the lock committed.

⚠ A dep whose tag commit trips an fsck POLICY check (an author line
with no email, a bad date) still resolves — those message ids are
passed as `warn`, because reading them as integrity failures made a
valid dep unresolvable for ever. And `core.fileMode` is PROBED, not
forced: on a filesystem that cannot store the exec bit (vfat, exfat,
9p, a WSL `/mnt/c`) forcing `true` refused every 755 entry in the tag
with no possible repair.

Cost: warm `cyrius deps` on one dep goes from ~7 ms to ~41 ms
(585-file dep) — a full `git fsck` is 11 ms typical, 70 ms on the
largest dep in the ecosystem. The whole 138-checkout store verifies
in ~6.3 s.

[[entries]]
name = "object_directive"
description = "object; — emit ELF .o relocatable output (kernel_mode = 3)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`object;` at the top of a .cyr file triggers ELF .o relocatable
output (sets `kernel_mode = 3`). The compiler emits a proper
ELF object with sections:

  .text, .data, .rodata, .symtab, .strtab, .rela.text

Functions become `STT_FUNC`/`STB_GLOBAL` symbols. Fixup entries
convert to relocations:

  type 0 (var addr)     → R_X86_64_64 against .data
  type 1 (string addr)  → R_X86_64_64 against .rodata
  type 2 (fn call)      → R_X86_64_PC32 against function symbol
  type 3 (fn pointer)   → R_X86_64_64 against function symbol

PIC codegen for `object;` mode uses `LEA [rip+disp32]` +
`R_X86_64_PC32` relocs — eliminates `DT_TEXTREL` (glibc 2.38+
refuses `dlopen` of TEXTREL binaries).

Top-level code (enum values, global var inits) wraps in a
callable `_cyrius_init` function that C launchers must invoke
before any application function.

[[entries]]
name = "compiler_env_vars"
description = "Compile-time env vars — target selection, IR pipeline, regalloc, profiling"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc reads several `CYRIUS_*` environment variables at startup
to switch target / enable optimizations / dump diagnostics.
Default-off — set at compile time, no source-code annotation
needed.

== TARGET SELECTION ==

  CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1     Windows PE32+ output (cycc native)
  CYRIUS_MACHO=1          Mach-O x86_64 output
  CYRIUS_MACHO_ARM=1      Mach-O aarch64 output
  CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1   AGNOS ring-3 userspace ABI (v6.0.x); with
                          `src/main_cx.cyr` it builds the agnos `cycc_cx`,
                          which compiles on agnos since 6.7.16 and reads
                          its own `CYRIUS_*` environment there (the first
                          256 entries; TT-4 – TT-10 — compiler_binaries)

Cross-compile dispatch hooks. `cyrius build --aarch64` sets
target via the appropriate cross-compiler binary; the env vars
are mostly used by the cross-compiler binaries themselves.
⚠ 6.7.11 (TL-10): `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` / `CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL` are
read by `src/main.cyr` ONLY (the aarch64 forks read `CYRIUS_KERNEL`;
the PE and both Mach-O forks read none of the three) — agnos and
x86_64 bare-metal images build from an x86_64 Linux host. The
Windows and macOS CLIs refuse `--agnos`,
`--target=x86_64-bare-metal-elf` and `--target=aarch64-bare-metal-elf`
by name, rc 1, before compiling (`error: --agnos is not available
from the Windows CLI: cycc.exe emits PE only (build agnos /
bare-metal images on Linux)`; macOS: `… cycc emits Mach-O only …`).
Since 6.7.12 (D6 — TL-30) the aarch64 Linux CLI refuses `--agnos` and
`--target=x86_64-bare-metal-elf` by name too (through 6.7.11 it DROPPED
`--agnos` and wrote an aarch64 Linux binary at rc 0) and labels its
builds `[aarch64]`. And ONE EMIT TARGET PER RUN (D20 — TL-10): any two
of `--aarch64`, `--win`, `--agnos`, `--target=<v>` are refused by name
(`error: --win and --aarch64 select two emit targets; pass one`, rc 1);
`--aarch64 --target=aarch64-bare-metal-elf` is the one accepted pair.
Since 6.7.13 (TL-2) the aarch64 Linux CLI refuses `--win` by name (`error:
--win is not available from the aarch64 Linux CLI: its cycc emits aarch64
only (build PE images on x86_64 Linux or Windows)`, rc 1, nothing written —
it wrote an aarch64 ELF named `.exe` through 6.7.12), and there
`--aarch64` / `--target=aarch64-bare-metal-elf` run the host's own cycc.
Since 6.7.14 (TL-1) both macOS CLIs refuse `--win` by name (`error: --win
is not available from the macOS CLI: cycc emits Mach-O only (build PE
images on x86_64 Linux or Windows)`, rc 1, nothing written — neither
Mach-O compiler fork reads `CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`, so through 6.7.13 they
wrote a Mach-O named `.exe` at rc 0): PE images are built on x86_64
Linux or Windows only. And the aarch64 Linux CLI's plain resolve reads a
`[deps.X] target` arch axis as aarch64 (TL-2: `"aarch64"` / `"arm64"`
deps vendored, `"x86_64"` / `"x86"` skipped, as `--aarch64` does).

== GENERICS (monomorphization) ==

  CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0     Opt OUT of generics monomorphization. As of
                         v6.4.0 generics (fns + structs) are DEFAULT-ON;
                         set =0 to disable. (Was opt-in =1 pre-v6.4.0.)
                         A debug flag: no type-argument list is read or
                         refused and a generic struct is its base. Since
                         6.7.16 every driver reads it BEFORE pass 1 (FF-50:
                         cx, the native aarch64 and the aarch64 Mach-O
                         drivers read it after, so their pass 1 refused
                         lists the others stepped), and a return, a
                         by-value or union field and a slice element step
                         their list under it (FF-51: `fn g(): Result<i64,
                         Pt>` was "expected '{', got '<'" there). Since 6.7.17
                         an explicit `g<i32>(3)` (D7) and a list on a
                         non-generic (D102) are refused by name under
                         the flag, as the default build refuses them.

== CODEGEN MODE ==

  CYRIUS_PIE=1           Position-independent codegen (ET_DYN,
                         ASLR-able). Mirrors the --pie flag. x86_64
                         RIP-relative + aarch64 adrp/add. (v6.1.6/.8)
                         A Linux ELF mode: neither Mach-O compiler
                         reads it (an exported CYRIUS_PIE=1 is inert
                         on both Macs — arm64 Mach-O is always
                         MH_PIE; the x86_64 macOS CLI refuses `--pie`
                         by name since 6.7.13, D43 — TL-3), and
                         cycc.exe has no PIE mode.

== TS/TSX → JS EMIT (--emit-js) ==

  CYRIUS_JSX_PRAGMA=NAME Override the JSX lowering pragma (default
                         `h`). When set, the standalone `h` runtime
                         prelude is suppressed (the consumer supplies
                         its own pragma runtime, e.g. React.createElement).
                         (v6.1.11)

== IR PIPELINE ==

  CYRIUS_IR (unset)  IR off (default — emit raw bytes via E*/E3*)
  CYRIUS_IR=1        IR recording on, raw emit also runs in parallel
  CYRIUS_IR=2        IR recording + BB construction (no opt passes)
  CYRIUS_IR=3        IR + opt passes (LASE + const-fold + bitmap-
                     liveness DCE + DSE + fixed-point driver) +
                     codebuf patching of eliminated nodes

  CYRIUS_DCE_CAP=N   Bisection cap — kill at most N IR nodes via DCE
  CYRIUS_DSE_CAP=N   Bisection cap — kill at most N IR nodes via DSE

DCE/DSE caps help bisect codegen-vs-IR divergence: start
unbounded, narrow to the first cap that introduces the bug.
The opt passes only run under CYRIUS_IR=3; the lower modes are
recording-only diagnostics.

STORAGE (6.7.14, IN-1): the IR is one `alloc()`'d arena taken when
CYRIUS_IR switches recording on (a default build never takes it),
growing 2x from 262,144 nodes / 32,768 blocks. The only limits are
runaway ceilings — `error: IR node table exceeds 64M nodes`, `error:
IR block table exceeds 16M blocks` (and `error: cannot allocate the IR
arena (CYRIUS_IR)`). `IR node buffer full` / `IR block table full`
(the old 1,048,576-node / 131,072-block heap-band caps) are gone: an
IR build of anything the default build accepts succeeds (the filed
3.0 MB program, 400 fns x 500 statements, needed 1,405,628 nodes).
⚠ CYRIUS_IR=3 is still OPT-IN, not release-grade: since IN-2 every
emitter records a node (an unrecorded emit belonged to the node before
it and died with it — a signed narrow field's `movsx`, a field store's
`lea`; ST-1: a float literal's `movabs`, so `q.f = 2.5;` stored
through 2.5's bits — the f64 field store through a pointer that
faulted). The last corpus file that failed under it,
`derive/derive_enum_inside_ifdef.tcyr`, passes since 6.7.15 (IT-1): a
const fold over a dead store NOP-filled its own immediate (`var r = 0;
r = r + 10; r = r + 100; return r;` → `b8 0a 50 b8 64`, a fault — the
dead-store pass had marked `r = 0`'s store eliminated, the fold wrote
`mov eax, 10` across it, and the deferred NOP fill landed in the new
immediate); the fold now consumes every eliminated node inside its span
(`_ir_fold_consume_span`). It returns 110 under IR=3 (it faulted —
reproduced on 6.6.20, 6.7.0 and 6.7.13). And since 6.7.15 (IT-2) a gate
runs the WHOLE `.tcyr`
corpus under `CYRIUS_IR=3` + `CYRIUS_DCE=1` together —
tests/gates/ir-opt/ir3_dce_corpus.sh, a serial leg of check.sh (~45 –
70 s): every file must compile and exit 0, its expected-failure list
may only shrink, and it is EMPTY. The pair matters: the IR=3 seam
compacts code before FIXUP reads parse-time positions, and only
`CYRIUS_DCE=1` acts on what FIXUP concludes, so a defect of the pair
(6.7.14's `_wp_fnr_shift`) passed every per-mode leg. Measure with IR=3;
ship the default pipeline. `CYRIUS_IR=2`'s dump shows a hole
(`PUSH, POP_A, ST64` for a field store whose lea is invisible);
`CYRIUS_DCE_CAP=0` / `CYRIUS_DSE_CAP=0` (and `CYRIUS_FOLD_OFF=1` for the
fold) name the pass.

== REGALLOC PEEPHOLE (v5.6.20+) ==

  CYRIUS_REGALLOC_AUTO_CAP=N   Auto-enable picker for first N fns only
                               (-1 = uncapped, default; 0 = picker off)
  CYRIUS_REGALLOC_DUMP=1       Per-fn interval dump for picker debug
  CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=N Cap total assignments per fn (bisection)

The post-body peephole assigns up to 5 callee-saved regs
(rbx, r12-r15) to hot user-locals via Poletto-Sarkar linear-scan.
Bars `&local` slots from assignment via the safety scan. v5.8.16
§8 highlights the picker's interaction with slice high-half
slots — see field_notes/compiler/v5_8.

== DIAGNOSTICS ==

  CYRIUS_PROF=1    Per-fn compile-time profiling
  CYRIUS_STATS=1   Final stats dump (token count, fn count,
                   fixup count, code+data sizes, alloc usage)

== PIN DRIFT (v5.11.57) ==

  CYRIUS_NO_WARN_PIN_DRIFT=1   Suppress the cycc-side warning
                               that fires when cwd's
                               `cyrius.cyml [package].cyrius`
                               pin doesn't match cycc's
                               compile-time version. Default:
                               warning enabled.

  CYRIUS_STRICT_PIN=1          Upgrade pin-drift warning to a
                               hard exit (code 1) with
                               `error: cyrius.cyml pins X.Y.Z
                               but cycc is X.Y.W — toolchain
                               drift (CYRIUS_STRICT_PIN)`. CI
                               gating path. `cyrius build
                               --strict-pin` sets this for the
                               cycc child automatically. Since
                               6.7.12 (D5) every host's compiler
                               runs the check — cycc.exe and
                               both macOS compilers too (it was
                               Linux-only, silent elsewhere).

  Both detect via the new `_check_cyml_pin_drift()` in
  `src/frontend/lex.cyr`. Surface drift regardless of wrapper
  state — the iron-boot papercut filing 2026-05-16 found that
  `~/.cyrius/bin/cyrius` had been frozen at .25-embedded since
  2026-05-12 (install.sh `_rebuild_stale` missed
  `version_str.cyr` transitive dep), and consumers saw `cyrius
  --version: 5.11.25` even when pinning .55+. cycc IS current
  per self-host discipline, so cycc-side detection bypasses the
  stale wrapper.

== INCLUDE OVERLAY (6.7.6) ==

  CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY=DIR  An `include "lib/<rest>"` tries DIR/<rest>
                          FIRST. Set by `cyrius` for its own compiles in
                          local mode (build/local-deps/lib) and under
                          `--locked` with no vendored lib/
                          (build/locked-deps/lib); never set it by hand
                          (an inherited value is removed). Ignored unless
                          relative, free of `..`, and at most 480 bytes.
                          See cyrius_cyml_git_first_local_switch_v676.

== DCE (v5.11.x; compaction v6.5.72; kernels / WX=0 6.7.11) ==

  CYRIUS_DCE=1            REMOVE the dead fns (whole-program
                          compaction) on three x86_64 Linux ELF
                          layouts: a plain W^X executable (since
                          v6.5.72), a `kernel;` image — ELF32
                          multiboot1 and CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1 —
                          and a CYRIUS_WX=0 executable (both since
                          6.7.11; 6.6.20 – 6.7.10 declined them and
                          only NOP-filled). Everywhere else it
                          NOP-fills in place (size unchanged):
                          `--pie` (a `--pie` kernel included),
                          `shared;`, aarch64, Mach-O, PE — the note
                          names the decline. Off by default; the
                          dead-fn DETECTION runs always and
                          produces `note: N unreachable fns (M
                          bytes — set CYRIUS_DCE=1 to eliminate)`;
                          under the flag `dead code eliminated`, or
                          `compaction declined on x86_64 ELF
                          (<reason>)` on a declining layout.
  CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1    Per-fn `  dead: <name>` list.

  ⛔ This block said "NOP-fill … binary size unchanged" from v5.11.x
  to 6.7.11 — stale since v6.5.72.
  On a kernel image and CYRIUS_WX=0 the data FOLLOWS the code, so
  `.bss` / `.rodata` move down with it, by a multiple of 16 (an array
  keeps its 16-byte alignment; up to 8 zero bytes pad the end of
  `.text`), and every data address in the code moves with them. Under
  W^X the data segment stays where it was. A dead fn KEPT because the
  length decoder cannot size one of its instructions (an `in` / `out`
  in an `asm` block) keeps its body; its calls / tail calls into
  removed fns become `ud2; ud2; nop` and its `&fn` of a removed fn 0.
  Any other rel32 from it into removed code refuses the build by name
  (`error: dead-code elimination: the rel32 at code offset N …
  refusing to emit`).

  6.7.14 (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] IN-3 - IN-8, IN-18):
  - No dead-fn count makes compaction give up (IN-4). Through 6.7.13
    more than 4,096 dead-code runs declined the WHOLE pass ("more than
    4096 dead-code runs, the repair registry cap" — kybernet's
    agnos-init went 248,440 -> 1,574,640 B); the run and switch-table
    registries now grow, contiguous dead fns cost one run, and a
    decline means an alloc failed: `… could not grow (alloc failed)`.
    The same holds under CYRIUS_IR=3 (NOP runs, switch tables).
  - A dead fn whose code range holds a LIVE fn's start is kept whole
    and still reported unreachable (IN-5): an inline generic instance
    is emitted inside the fn that first needs it, so ranges nest, and
    a generic instance nested in a dead forwarder is kept (it was
    removed with it — `lang/generic_instance_inline_binding.tcyr`).
  - A redefined fn's LOSING body keeps nothing alive (IN-6): what only
    it calls is unreachable and removed.
  - On x86 what reaches a fn is a `call` / `jmp rel32` that STARTS AN
    INSTRUCTION (IN-18) — the call graph was a byte scan, so an E8
    inside an imm32 / disp32 landing on a fn start kept a dead fn alive
    and the dead set moved with the global count. Code the length
    decoder cannot walk exactly keeps the byte scan (a fn holding a raw
    `asm { 0x.. }` byte, the top level once it holds one, a fn holding
    a switch's jump table, VEX / `in` / `out`), so nothing called is
    counted dead. The `note: N unreachable fns` count can RISE against
    6.7.13 for the same source — re-measure, do not pin it.
  - `.text`'s pad after compacted code is zeros (IN-3; it kept
    pre-compaction bytes). `CYRIUS_SYMS` is written after compaction
    (IN-8): it lists the compacted addresses, without the removed fns,
    and a kept dead fn where it sits — resolve a crash address against
    the map of the binary that crashed.

  6.7.15 (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] IT-9, IT-6):
  - The DETECTION's seed pass is linear (IT-9). For every call / jump
    (x86 E8 / E9, aarch64 BL / B) landing on a fn start it asked
    WHETHER the site lies inside any fn through a scan of the whole fn
    table, O(call sites × fns), and the detection runs on EVERY build
    (it makes the `note: N unreachable fns`), not only under
    CYRIUS_DCE=1. Now the fn ranges are packed once per FIXUP, sorted
    and merged, and each site is a binary search (`_dce_cover_build`,
    `_dce_in_any_fn`; exact by construction — the merge keeps a nesting
    range's OUTER end). Every output byte-identical (763 inputs × 12
    modes, the note and the `dead:` list included). Measured: a
    16,000-fn program's x86 FIXUP 9,934 → 114 ms; cycc's own
    self_compile min 1,980 → 1,278 ms on the lane's box — the one patch
    behind the release's −34.6 % on the same input. Gate:
    tests/gates/codegen/dce_seed_pass_linear.sh.
  - The x86 length decoder walks 6.7.15's register-naming asm operand
    forms (`lgdt [r12]`'s SIB, `lgdt [rbp]`'s disp8 0, REX.B / REX.R)
    with its generic ModR/M path (IT-6).

  Both arches (x86 + aarch64) since v5.11.59 — aarch64 gained
  the DCE pass + filter same slot. See `field_notes/compiler/
  retros/` for the cross-arch parity arc.

== EXECUTION COVERAGE (6.7.10) ==

  CYRIUS_COVERAGE=1       Per-fn hit counters, dumped when the program
                          exits (`0`, unset or empty = off; anything
                          else refused by name). Predefines
                          CYRIUS_COVERAGE for #ifdef. Refused for
                          kernel / EFI / `shared;` / `object;`.
  CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE=P  REQUIRED with the mode (no default; over
                          255 B warned of and refused): the file the
                          program APPENDS to at exit, fixed at compile
                          time — give an absolute path.
  CYRIUS_POISON=1         The poisoned allocator (lib/'s `#ifdef
                          CYRIUS_POISON` arms; predefines CYRIUS_POISON).
  See execution_coverage_mode_v6710.
  ONE RULE FOR THE THREE COMPILE-MODE SWITCHES (6.7.11, TL-8):
  CYRIUS_POISON, CYRIUS_BOUNDS and CYRIUS_COVERAGE — `1` on; `0`, empty
  or unset off; any other value refused by name (`<NAME> must be 0 or 1
  (or unset): <v>`, rc 1, nothing written), decided ONCE in the shared
  PREPROCESS (`_pp_env_rung`, before the first `#ifdef`). Until 6.7.11
  any set CYRIUS_POISON — `0`, empty, a word — turned poison ON (each
  fork read "is it set"). On Windows (cmd.exe, wine) an empty variable
  is unset. `cyrius pulsar` and the on-demand `cycc_cx` / `cxvm` builds
  run WITHOUT CYRIUS_POISON / CYRIUS_BOUNDS / CYRIUS_COVERAGE /
  CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE (a filtered envp; on Windows saved, cleared and
  restored around the spawn); the user's own compiles keep them (TL-9).

[[entries]]
name = "compiler_flags"
description = "cycc + cyrius command-line flags — --strict, --pie, --version, the TS modes (--lex-ts/--parse-ts/--emit-js), --aarch64, etc. (binary renamed cc5 → cycc at v6.0.0)."
category = "cyrius"
---
== cycc BINARY FLAGS == (the compiler binary; cc5 was its pre-v6.0.0 name)

  --version          Print "cycc X.Y.Z\n" and exit 0
  --strict           No effect since v6.3.2 (still accepted). A
                     REACHABLE undefined-fn reference is already a
                     hard error by default: no binary, exit 1.
  --allow-undef      Downgrade that error to a warning; the call
                     site is patched to `ud2; ud2; nop`, so the
                     binary SIGILLs (exit 132) when it is reached.
                     Rarely correct.

      Output on undef (default, re-verified 6.6.20):
          warning: undefined function 'X' (reachable call site)
          error: refusing to emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s) (pass --allow-undef to downgrade)

  --pie              Position-independent codegen — ET_DYN, ASLR-able
                     (x86_64 RIP-relative `lea`; aarch64 `adrp`/`add`).
                     Also via CYRIUS_PIE=1 env. (v6.1.6 x86 / v6.1.8 aarch64.)
                     `cyrius build --pie` is refused by name from the
                     Windows CLI (cycc.exe has no PIE mode; 6.7.12,
                     TL-28) and from the x86_64 macOS CLI (`error:
                     --pie is not available from the macOS x86_64
                     CLI: its cycc writes a fixed-address Mach-O (no
                     MH_PIE mode)`; 6.7.13, D43 — TL-3); the arm64
                     macOS CLI accepts it as already met (every arm64
                     Mach-O is MH_PIE, flags 0x00200084).

  TS/TSX FRONTEND MODES (take a `.ts`/`.tsx` path arg, else read stdin;
  read by EVERY compiler but cycc_cx since 6.7.15 — 6.7.15's D38, PL-13 —
  with x86_64 Linux's exit codes, diagnostics and JS bytes; through 6.7.14
  every other compiler ignored them and compiled the TS as cyrius):
  --lex-ts [file]    Lex only; exit 0 (clean) / 1 (lex error).
  --parse-ts [file]  Lex + parse; exit 0 (both clean) / 1.
  --emit-js [file]   Lex + parse + emit JS to stdout (v6.1.11): strips
                     types/interfaces/aliases/`as`/generics, lowers JSX
                     to an `h()` pragma (CYRIUS_JSX_PRAGMA, default `h`,
                     + a standalone runtime prelude when JSX present),
                     ESM passthrough with type-only export pruning.
                     Exits non-zero on an AST-walk integrity failure.

  CLI parsing: cycc reads `/proc/self/cmdline` and walks every arg,
  matching by prefix — `--st` strict, `--ve` version, `--pie`,
  `--le` lex-ts, `--pa` parse-ts, `--em` emit-js. For the TS modes
  the next non-flag arg is the input path; otherwise cycc reads
  source on stdin. The other drivers read the TS flags the same way
  (`src/frontend/ts/driver_args.cyr`, 6.7.15): the aarch64 Linux
  drivers and the Linux-hosted Windows cross compiler through the same
  /proc read, both Mach-O compilers from argv on the entry stack, and
  `cycc.exe` from GetCommandLineW split as CommandLineToArgvW splits it
  (a quoted spaced path and a non-ASCII name work). Since 6.7.16 (TL-9)
  the /proc drivers — cycc, cycc_aarch64, the aarch64 native cycc, the
  Linux-hosted cycc_win, cycc_cx — read the WHOLE command line
  (src/common/util.cyr's `_cmdline_read`, a 2 MiB mapping read to EOF),
  so every flag and a TS path anywhere on the line take effect; a
  command line of 2,097,152 bytes or more (only under a raised `ulimit
  -s`) is refused by name, exit 1: `error: the command line is longer
  than 2097151 bytes (/proc/self/cmdline is read whole; a cut copy is
  never scanned)` (a failed map or read: `cannot map 2 MiB to read the
  command line` / `cannot read the command line`). Through 6.7.15 it was
  ONE 255-byte read: a path past byte 255 was cut and opened cut, a flag
  past it dropped (`cycc <300-byte arg> --version` compiled stdin).
  `cycc_cx --syntax-only` stops after the parse with its error status,
  printing the `large static data` / capacity advisories, and writes no
  `.cyx` since 6.7.16 (TL-10, TL-10r); its full-compile refusal
  (`undefined function(s) called (cx backend)`) lists each fn once.

== `cyrius build` FLAGS ==

  --aarch64          Use cycc_aarch64 (the cross compiler) on an
                     x86_64 host; on an aarch64 Linux host the host's
                     own cycc, so the flag is a no-op there (6.7.13,
                     TL-2)
  --no-deps          Skip dep auto-prepend (compile only the
                     given source against bare stdlib)
  -D NAME            Inject `#define NAME` at top of bundled
                     source (feature-flag style)
  -v / --verbose     Show resolved compiler path + bundled-source
                     temp file path
  -q / --quiet       Suppress non-error stderr output

== `cyrius check` FLAGS ==

  --with-deps        Include `[deps]` distfiles in the syntax
                     check (default: standalone)
  --no-redirect      (with --with-deps; 6.7.12, TL-25) check the file
                     itself — skip the legacy module redirect that
                     checked src/main.cyr in place of a file with no
                     `include`; cyrius-lsp runs it in a project (an
                     older pinned CLI refuses it, and the server
                     retries without)

== `cyrius fmt` / `cyrius lint` / `cyrius doc` FLAGS ==

  --check            Exit-code-only mode — non-zero on drift,
                     silent on no-drift (matches Rust/Go fmt
                     conventions; aligned at v5.8.x cycle).
                     Suitable for CI gates.

== `cyrlint` FLAGS (the linter) ==

  --strict           Exit 2 if any warning is present (CI gate).
  --exit-with-count  Exit code = warning count (legacy; opt-in).
  --strict-deferrals Exit 2 if any UNTRACKED deferral-language
                     marker is present (v6.0.63). cyrlint flags
                     deferred / broken-work markers — TODO, FIXME,
                     SCAFFOLD, NOT_IMPLEMENTED, XXX (exact case) and
                     deferred, "for now", "not yet", "follow-up",
                     "later bite", "future bite", "out of scope"
                     (ANY case, any run of spaces, since v6.6.5) —
                     that are NOT cross-referenced by a CHANGELOG /
                     roadmap / docs/ / issue / "See " / version
                     (v6.6, v0.8.0 — any `v<digits>.<digit>` since
                     v6.6.5) pointer ON A LINE THE TERM TOUCHES.
                     v6.6.5: the unit is the COMMENT PARAGRAPH —
                     consecutive comment lines (and a trailing
                     comment plus the lines continuing it) are
                     joined, so `# a later` / `# bite` and
                     `# follow-` / `# up` are caught; a blank `#`,
                     a code line or a #skip-lint line ends it. A
                     pointer elsewhere in the paragraph does NOT
                     track the term (a block-wide pointer hid one of
                     the three mabda cases that motivated the join).
                     These use a SEPARATE counter
                     from warnings (so the legacy backlog doesn't
                     break the existing --strict gate); the count +
                     per-line notes go to STDERR (stdout's last line
                     stays "<n> warnings" for `tail -1` parsers),
                     non-failing by default. --strict-deferrals is
                     the hardening switch to flip once the backlog
                     is cross-referenced. Stops deferred work
                     rotting untracked in comments. `#skip-lint` on
                     a line exempts it from all rules.
  TWO RULES (6.7.11, TL-3), warnings like the rest:
    - a constant-offset `load8/16/32/64(&a + K)` / `store*(&a + K, ..)`
      past a bare `var a[N]`. Since 6.7.12 (TL-21) N is sized as the
      compiler sizes it: an integer literal, a `const NAME = <literal>;`,
      an enum constant with a literal value (bare or `E.X`), or `+ - *`
      over those (a fn-local array is N BYTES rounded up to 8); a
      TOP-LEVEL bare `var g[N]` is checked too, at N × 8 bytes, in every
      fn whose local or parameter does not shadow it; the warning names
      the spelling and its value. Typed / scalar locals shadow and are
      not checked. (6.7.11 took a literal N only and skipped top-level
      arrays.)
    - a `syscall(SYS_WRITE|1, …)` / `sys_write` / `_aw_write` whose
      literal length argument is not the literal's byte count, counted
      as the lexer STORES it (a raw byte 1 — an em dash 3 —, a
      two-character escape 1, `\xHH` 1, `\u` its UTF-8 length).
    Trailers: `# lint: offset-ok` (the overrun rule only, on the
    call's name line) and `#skip-lint` (both).

== `cyrius doc` ADDITIONAL ==

  ⛔ CORRECTED v6.6.5. This block advertised `--serve [port]` —
  "Serve generated HTML docs locally on lib/http_server.cyr
  (default port 8080)". THAT MODE HAS NEVER EXISTED.
  programs/cyrdoc.cyr has no serve path; measured on 6.6.4,
  `cyrius doc --serve` produced no output and exited 0, and
  `cyrius doc --serve 8080` said "cannot read: 8080". Since
  v6.6.5 an undeclared flag is an error that names the token,
  so the spelling now fails loudly instead of quietly.
  The same pass removed this file's claim that `cyrius lint`
  takes `--check` (it never has — cyrlint opened a file called
  "--check"); lint's real flags are listed above.

  --check            Report undocumented public fns; the exit code is
                     that count, CLAMPED TO 255 since v6.6.5 (it used
                     to be returned raw, and an exit status is 8 bits,
                     so 256 undocumented fns exited 0 — a clean bill
                     of health for the worst file — while 257 exited 1).

== `cyrius api-surface` FLAGS ==

  --update           Regenerate `docs/api-surface.snapshot` from
                     current source. No flag → diff against
                     committed snapshot, exit 1 on drift.
  --snapshot=PATH    Diff against an alternate snapshot file.
  --snapshot PATH    Same, space form (accepted since v6.6.5).
  --scope project    Scan src/ only, excluding the vendored lib/.
                     `--scope=project` is the equivalent attached form.

  ARITY FORMS in the snapshot: `name/N`; `name/MIN-MAX` for a fn with
  defaulted parameters (6.7.7); `name/MIN-` for a variadic fn, an open
  upper bound (D22, 6.7.12 — TL-9; `fn va(n, ...)` is `va/1-`).

  ⚠ Before v6.6.5 EVERY unrecognized token here was silently
  ignored — `--updat`, `--scope=projct`, the space form of
  `--snapshot`, and any stray positional — so the tool diffed
  against the DEFAULT snapshot and reported a clean surface for
  a question nobody had asked. They are errors now.

== `cyrius capacity` FLAGS ==

  --check            Exit non-zero on cap overflow — for CI gates
  --json             Machine-readable output

== `cyrius deps` FLAGS ==

  --no-lock          Don't write/check cyrius.lock
  --lock             Re-hash lib/ into cyrius.lock, KEEPING the commit
                     pins (v6.6.4 — it used to drop every `commit\t`
                     line, after which a repointed tag was accepted)
  --verify           Re-hash every vendored lib/ file against its
                     lock line; exit non-zero on mismatch. (Tag pins —
                     the `commit\t` lines — are checked at RESOLVE time,
                     not here.) Reads the whole file
                     since v6.6.4 — a lock past 64 KB used to stop
                     verifying at the cut)
  --relock           Accept a stdlib leaf whose pinned snapshot
                     disagrees with the lock under an UNCHANGED
                     [package].cyrius (otherwise refused, v6.6.4)

== `cyrius distlib` FLAGS (v6.5.8) ==

  --all              Regenerate the base bundle AND every
                     `[lib.<name>]` profile in the manifest
  --check            Same coverage as --all, but regenerate into a
                     sibling temp and byte-compare — verify, don't
                     write. CI-shaped.
  --modular          Modular bundle layout
  Naming a profile alongside --all/--check is an error, as is any
  unknown option (both used to fall through and "succeed").
  THE OWNER RULE (6.7.11, TL-4) — one rule for a name undefined on
  every target or on some: skip an in-unit declarer; a fold counts
  only when nothing else declares it; the one dispatcher whose peer
  declares it; else the one non-peer declarer; else REFUSED naming
  every candidate, no sidecar written (`'X' is undefined on every
  target, and more than one stdlib leaf could own it (a, b) — include
  the right leaf from a bundled module. Sidecar NOT written`). lib/ has
  NO live tie since 6.7.12 (D11 — LIB-1): `lib/ws_server.cyr` includes
  `lib/ws.cyr`, the nine `WS_*` are ws.cyr's enum constants, and distlib
  records `ws`; `distlib_owner_rule_shared.sh`'s ALLOW list is empty and
  its row W pins the old repro (yantra's distlib converges). A sidecar may
  record `syscalls` before an agnos-arm leaf (`O_*` -> io, `PROT_*` /
  `MAP_*` -> mmap, `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` -> chrono).

== `cyrius coverage` FLAGS ==

  --full             Scan the vendored stdlib in lib/ instead of
                     the project's own src/
  --min <pct>        Exit non-zero below the floor (CI gate); under
                     --exec it gates the EXECUTED percentage
  --exec[=<glob>]    (6.7.10) EXECUTION coverage: every .tcyr and
                     every program the globs name (`[coverage] exec`,
                     replaced by --exec=<glob>, repeatable) built with
                     CYRIUS_COVERAGE=1 and RUN; adds `E/T executed`
                     and `Functions executed: E/T (p%)`; -v lists the
                     unexecuted fns. See execution_coverage_mode_v6710.
  <dir>              Positional subfolder callout (v6.5.8) — scan
                     that directory. Previously dropped silently.

[[entries]]
name = "lsp_and_plugins"
description = "cyrius-lsp + .lsp.json + MacCracken/cyrius-plugins marketplace"
category = "cyrius"
---
== `cyrius-lsp` (Language Server) ==

JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdin/stdout. Source at
`programs/cyrius-lsp.cyr`. Built via `cyrius lsp` →
`~/.cyrius/bin/cyrius-lsp`. Promoted to `[release].bins` at
v5.7.39.

Capabilities (current):
  initialize                    — handshake
  textDocument/didOpen          — compile + return diagnostics
  textDocument/didChange        — recompile + return diagnostics
  textDocument/didSave          — recompile + return diagnostics
  textDocument/definition       — cross-file go-to-def (v5.7.39)
  textDocument/documentSymbol   — symbol outline (v5.7.39)
  shutdown                      — exit cleanly

Diagnostics are produced by invoking cycc on the buffer and
parsing its `error:` / `warning:` lines back into LSP `Diagnostic`
records. Since 6.7.12 (TL-25) the server parses `<file>:<line>[:<col>]:
<msg>` (plus the older `<line>: msg` and bare `error: msg` forms),
starts the range at cycc's COLUMN (to the end of that source line) and
strips the location from the message; publishes each diagnostic on the
URI of the FILE IT NAMES (an included file's error lands on that file;
an empty list clears a file a recompile no longer names; a name that
resolves to no file stays on the open file's line 0 with its location in
the message); handles `.cyr`, `.tcyr`, `.bcyr`, `.fcyr`, `.scyr` and
`.smcyr`; and in a project runs `cyrius check --with-deps --no-redirect
<file>` — falling back to the flagless form for an older pinned CLI,
since the server drives the PINNED CLI. OUTSIDE a project (6.7.13,
TL-14) the server checks a loose file as `cyrius check <file>` from its own
working directory, the file named relative to it; with cycc alone it feeds
cycc `#@incdir <dir>` + `#@srcline`, then the file, through a pipe. So a
sibling `include` resolves (through 6.7.12 it resolved against the
server's cwd). A file outside the working directory gets no marker, as
`cyrius check` writes none. Since 6.7.14 (D47 = D54, the user,
2026-10-16; TL-8) a file with no cyrius.cyml at or above it reads NO pin
of the server's working directory: its `cyrius check <file>` runs with
`CYRIUS_RESOLVED=1` (no version redirect) and `CYRIUS_NO_WARN_PIN_DRIFT=1`
(no drift warning about that pin), and inherited entries of either name
are dropped (each reader takes the first). So under an uninstalled pin it
publishes `[]` (6.7.13 showed that pin's error on line 1), and under an
INSTALLED different pin it is checked by the toolchain the server found
(6.7.13 ran the pinned CLI on it). A project's own files keep their pin.
To see the working directory's pin applied to a loose file, run `cyrius
check <abs file>` from the project.

A PROJECT FILE IS CHECKED THROUGH ITS COMPILE UNIT (6.7.16, TL-15, TT-2; the
fragment issue's decision, the user 2026-10-19). Each project file takes one
mode:
  - SELF — an entry, checked as itself, named relative to the root;
  - FRAGMENT — checked through the entry that reaches it (`[build] entry` first,
    then the fewest include hops, then path order), publishing on that unit's
    files only;
  - PEER — a file only a Windows / agnos / macOS / EFI entry includes, checked
    under CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1 / CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1 / CYRIUS_MACHO=1 /
    CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1, the open file published only (an x86_64 Linux server
    selects these; any other server only its own target);
  - NOTE — only an aarch64 or cx entry includes it: one Information diagnostic,
    no compile;
  - SKIP — listed in the manifest's `[lsp] skip`: an empty list, no compile.
A public lib/ module is checked as `include "lib/<m>.cyr"` alone through a
private 0700 include file (no [deps] prepend); a lib/ peer (the stdlib gate's
rule) through its nearest public includer. A clean fragment of a unit with
errors elsewhere gets one Information note (`checked as part of src/main.cyr,
which has 2 errors in other files`), and every publish of a unit, from any of
its files, republishes each open FRAGMENT / PEER document of it with the list
its own publish would give it now (TT-2) — so the note stays while the unit has
errors elsewhere, and opening another file of the unit never clears it.
Location-less warnings and `<source>` land on the entry, location-less errors on
the open file. One compile per unit until a dependency changes on disk (a
signature over the closure, every lib/ file, the manifest, the lock and the
binaries); the compiler's output is read whole (64 MiB cap; it kept 8,191
bytes); go-to-definition resolves includes from the project root and indexes the
entry; an inherited CYRIUS_TARGET_* / CYRIUS_MACHO* never reaches a project
child; each project file spawns the CLI twice (`cyrius build --print-config`,
then the check). Through 6.7.15 a fragment was compiled ALONE — every name its
entry supplies a false error (the filing counted 199 false diagnostics in
cycc's stderr for src/frontend/parse_ctrl.cyr). The
guide: docs/guides/editor-integration.md, "How a file's unit is chosen".
MACOS (6.7.16, TL-11): the server reads HOME / CYRIUS_HOME through getenv,
builds its children's environment from `env_block` (the whole environment; it
cut at 32,767 B), and finds its own directory through /proc/self/exe on Linux
and argv(0) + fcntl(F_GETPATH) on macOS — so it runs on macOS (both macOS
tarballs ship it; it published nothing there) but not on Windows (cycc_win
refuses it). A server started with a std fd closed gives its child an open
/dev/null there (TL-12).

== `.lsp.json` (project-root manifest) ==

Editor-agnostic shape (same as gopls' `.lsp.json` convention).
Maps file extensions to language servers:

    {
      "cyrius": {
        "command": "cyrius-lsp",
        "args": [],
        "extensionToLanguage": {
          ".cyr":   "cyrius",
          ".tcyr":  "cyrius",
          ".bcyr":  "cyrius",
          ".fcyr":  "cyrius",
          ".scyr":  "cyrius",
          ".smcyr": "cyrius"
        }
      }
    }

Don't drop this file into each Cyrius repo — install the plugin
instead (next section). Per-repo `.lsp.json` was the original
wiring; the plugin extracts it to user scope.

== `MacCracken/cyrius-plugins` (Claude Code marketplace) ==

Sibling repo to `MacCracken/cyrius`. Houses the Claude Code
plugin manifests (`.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` +
`plugins/cyrius-lsp/`) so editor wiring lives outside the
language repo.

Install once at user scope:

    /plugin marketplace add MacCracken/cyrius-plugins
    /plugin install cyrius-lsp@cyrius-plugins

Tags follow `<plugin-name>--v<X.Y.Z>` convention (e.g.
`cyrius-lsp--v0.1.0`). Per-plugin tags so plugins version
independently. CI in the plugins repo runs `claude plugin
validate` + cross-checks marketplace.json ↔ plugin.json
version agreement on every PR; release.yml fires on tag push,
publishes a GitHub Release with auto-notes.

[[entries]]
name = "helper_scripts"
description = "cyriusly + cyrius-init.sh + cyrius-port.sh + cyrius-watch.sh + cyrius-repl.sh + cyrius-prompt-info"
category = "cyrius"
---
Shell scripts that wrap cycc / `cyrius` for common workflows.
Listed in cyrius.cyml's `[release].scripts`.

== `cyriusly` — toolchain orchestration + status ==

The Swiss-army-knife wrapper. Sub-commands:

  cyriusly setup              Install ~/.cyrius/bin to PATH;
                              symlink toolchain binaries; ensure
                              cycc / cyrius / cyrius-lsp resolvable
  cyriusly status             Print installed binaries + versions
  cyriusly upgrade            Pull + rebuild from main
  cyriusly nightly            Soak loop + bench history snapshot

Also drops a starship.toml / p10k snippet so the shell prompt
shows the current Cyrius project's version + dep status. Glyph:
🌀 (cyclone) for cyrius, ॐ (Om) for packages.

== `cyrius-init.sh` — project scaffold ==

Invoked as `cyrius init <name>`. Creates `<name>/` with:
  cyrius.cyml          (manifest skeleton)
  src/main.cyr         (hello-world entry)
  tests/<name>.tcyr    (test harness)
  tests/<name>.bcyr    (bench harness)
  tests/<name>.fcyr    (fuzz harness)
  docs/development/    (roadmap / state / completed-phases stubs)
  README.md / VERSION  (version-bump-friendly defaults)
  .lsp.json            (DEPRECATED — install cyrius-plugins instead)

⚠ `_tests_rel` writes ALL THREE corpora flat under `tests/` — there
is no `tests/tcyr/`, `benches/` or `fuzz/` directory in a scaffolded
project. That mismatch against the discovery walkers is what produced
three separate "verb cannot see its own scaffolded artifact" bugs
(`test` v6.4.72, `bench` v6.4.78, `fuzz` v6.5.6); `tests/` is now in
every walker's search set and the walkers recurse (v6.5.7).
The generated `.tcyr` epilogue CLAMPS `assert_summary()` (v6.5.6):
a wait status is only 8 bits, so a suite with exactly 256 / 512 / 768
failing assertions used to truncate to 0 and report PASS. Templates
also emit `sys_exit_group(r)`, not `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` — seven
templates carried the per-thread form, which hangs any program that
has spawned a worker.

== `cyrius port` — port scaffold (rust, python) ==

Invoked as `cyrius port [--language=<lang>] [--dry-run] <dir>`.
NOT a bash shim: `scripts/shims/cyrius-port.sh` was deleted at
v6.2.40 and this is `programs/cyrius-init.cyr` in port mode
(the CLI prepends a `--__mode=port` sentinel). This entry said
"cyrius-port.sh" for eleven minors.

Moves the existing tree aside into `<lang>-old/`, counts its
lines, and scaffolds cyrius.cyml + src/ + docs/ + CI over the
top. It does NOT auto-translate source — the preserved tree is
a parity ORACLE you cross-check the hand-written port against,
which is why the scaffolded CLAUDE.md tells you not to edit it.

**v6.5.34 added the python arm.** Before that, `--language`
accepted a value and declined everything but rust, citing a
milestone ("planned for future v5.6.x") the toolchain had passed
a major line earlier.

  lang    marker file                          preserved as
  rust    Cargo.toml                           rust-old/
  python  pyproject.toml | setup.py | setup.cfg  python-old/

Any of the three Python markers counts: requiring PEP 621 alone
would decline the older half of the ecosystem.

⚠ THE FLAG IS THE SMALL HALF. The port TEMPLATES hardcoded
"Rust" and "rust-old/" in 17 places across 7 files, so a
flag-only fix scaffolds a Python project whose CLAUDE.md,
roadmap, state and getting-started all name `rust-old/` as the
parity oracle while the tree on disk is `python-old/`. Templates
now take `{SRC_LANG}` / `{OLD_DIR}` / `{SRC_LOC}`; adding the
next language is a branch in `_run_port` and nothing else.
`tests/gates/toolchain/port_language_arms.sh` axis 3 asserts a
Python scaffold contains no Rust vocabulary, with an
anti-vacuous companion proving the same grep DOES find it in a
Rust scaffold.

⚠ `_rw` was fail-open until v6.5.34: it returned -1 for a
template it could not render and no call site looked, so an
incomplete scaffold printed "Created <x>" for every file and
exited 0. Failures are now counted and both `init` and `port`
exit non-zero on a partial tree.

== `cyrius-watch.sh` — file watcher ==

Run as `cyrius-watch.sh [src] [out]` — the CLI has NO `watch` verb
(this entry said "Invoked as `cyrius watch`" until 6.7.12; since 6.7.14
the script's own usage line names the script, and no verb was added). Polls
`*.cyr` files via `find -newer` and recompiles on change. Defaults:
`src/main.cyr` → `build/app`. Interval via `CYRIUS_WATCH_INTERVAL` env
(default 1 second). Polling rather than inotify — simple, portable,
sufficient for dev workflows. Ctrl+C to stop.
SHIPPING (6.7.12, D4 — DW-6): it ships in BOTH macOS release tarballs
(every `[release] scripts` entry ships); both macOS builders read the
list through `scripts/release-scripts.sh`, as release.yml does for x86_64
Linux — it looks in `scripts/shims/` first, then `scripts/`, and fails on
a missing entry or an empty list. Since 6.7.14 (PL-9) every POSIX
tarball ships it — the aarch64-linux one too.
BUILDS (6.7.14, D60 — PL-8): each build, the first pass and the rebuild
loop, is `cyrius build "$SRC" "$OUT"` — the manifest's [deps] / includes
are prepended and `include` resolves relative to the entry file (the
`#@incdir` marker), the output is signed on macOS, the compiler's
diagnostic is shown, and a failed build KEEPS the previous output. The
CLI is `CYRIUS_BIN`, else `cyrius` on PATH, else
`${CYRIUS_HOME:-$HOME/.cyrius}/bin/cyrius` (none: `error: the cyrius CLI
not found (set CYRIUS_BIN, or put cyrius on PATH)`); `CYRIUS_CC` is
IGNORED, and the banner prints `cyrius: <path>`. Through 6.7.13 it ran
raw `cat "$SRC" | cycc > "$OUT" 2>/dev/null`: on Apple Silicon the
unsigned output was killed at launch (137), an entry-relative `include`
and the manifest's [deps] failed on every OS, the diagnostic was hidden
behind "COMPILE ERROR", and a failed build left an empty output. Gate:
tests/gates/toolchain/cyrius_watch_builds_like_build.sh.

== `cyrius-repl.sh` — expression REPL ==

Invoked as `cyrius repl`. Reads expressions on stdin, wraps each
in a minimal `fn main` template, compiles + runs, prints result.

== `cyrius-prompt-info` — shell-prompt status helper ==

Used by the cyriusly starship/p10k integration. Reads the
nearest cyrius.cyml + dep lockfile, emits a compact status
string for prompt rendering. Drops redundant "name (name)"
when project name == repo name (v5.8.1 polish).

== THE DEV AND RELEASE HELPERS ARE CYRIUS PROGRAMS (6.7.17 — CHANGELOG [6.7.17] TO1; the user's D38) ==

The helpers moved into `scripts/` on 2026-10-19 (Python and shell) are cyrius programs, each with the old script's
output on the same input (tests/gates/toolchain/dev_helpers_match_golden.sh, a golden per helper), and `scripts/`
holds no Python. Run them from the checkout's root:

  cyrius run programs/dev/regen_issue_index.cyr     # issues/README.md § Open queue from each **Placement:**
  cyrius run programs/dev/{ab,keepboth,probe,xb,envprobe}.cyr   # A/B timing, keep-both merges, probes, 4-target run, box inventory
  cyrius run programs/dev/fixpoint.cyr              # was build-cycc-verify.sh --fixpoint / --install
  cyrius run programs/folds/{sibci,run_ci,cymlcheck}.cyr        # a fold's CI isolated; the YAML GitHub reads
  cyrius run programs/release/{place,briefs,assemble,archive,roadmap_state}.cyr  # the release's document steps

The Workflow scripts (`scripts/release/{lanes,triage,docs}.js`) are input to the agent harness, not programs, and stay
in `scripts/release/` (D38). `programs/release/README.md` and `scripts/release/README.md` hold every step's options.

[[entries]]
name = "release_pipeline"
description = "release.yml + install.sh + cbt/pulsar.cyr — driven by [release] in cyrius.cyml"
category = "cyrius"
---
The release pipeline reads `[release]` from `cyrius.cyml` —
single source of truth for what ships. Adding a new binary
requires exactly one edit (the `bins` array); release.yml,
install.sh, and pulsar all pick it up automatically.

== `release.yml` (GitHub Actions) ==

Triggers on tag push (`vX.Y.Z`). Builds:
  - x86_64 Linux: cycc + every binary in `[release].bins`
  - aarch64 Linux: cross-compiles each binary in `[release].cross_bins`
    via cycc_aarch64
  - Windows PE: cross-compiles cycc_win + tools (when wired)
  - macOS Mach-O: cross-compiles via cycc_macho_arm

Stages binaries under `$STAGE/bin/`; `cross_bins` go under
`$STAGE/bin/` alongside the native (cycc_aarch64 in same dir as
cycc). v5.8.2 fixed a stage-path drift where cycc_aarch64 went to
`$STAGE/` instead of `$STAGE/bin/`.

THE aarch64-linux TARBALL (6.7.14, D59 — PL-9) ships every `[release]
bins` entry built for aarch64 with `./build/cycc_aarch64` (the native
aarch64 compiler, run through qemu-user's binfmt on the runner) and
arch-checked (`file | grep "ARM aarch64"`): the `cyrius` CLI (from
`cbt/cyrius.cyr`), cyrfmt, cyrlint, cyrdoc, cyrius-lsp and the rest
(`programs/<bin>.cyr`; an entry with no source fails the step by name).
Through 6.7.13 it shipped cycc, cyrsign, cyrius-init and the cyriusly
shell script only — no `cyrius build` / `test` / `repl` on an ARM binary
install. cybs is the one bin NOT shipped: it is the x86_64 assembly the
29 KB seed assembles and has no aarch64 form. The three `[release]
scripts` ship through `scripts/release-scripts.sh`, which all four POSIX
builders call; `bin/cyriusly` is the compiled version manager there too.
`ts_test_runner` ships there (6.7.14's D59, kept by 6.7.15's D54) and
WORKS since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's D38 — PL-13): the aarch64 compilers carry
the TS front end, so `--parse-ts` runs it (pi, a throwaway HOME: `0/1
passed (1 failed)` rc 1 → `1/1 passed` rc 0). Through 6.7.14 the aarch64
drivers skipped the flag and compiled the TypeScript as cyrius, so the
runner failed every `.ts` file. `scripts/cross-os-selfhost.sh` checks
`--parse-ts` / `--emit-js` on ecb, ach, pi and cass (`TS_FAIL`
otherwise).

THE macOS AND WINDOWS TARBALLS (6.7.16, TL-11) build every `[release]
bins` entry too, through `scripts/release-bins.sh <macos-arm64|macos-x86_64|
windows> <bin-dir> <compiler>` — cyrius.cyml's list read as release.yml
reads it, every source checked before compiling, each image arch-checked
from its bytes (Mach-O magic + cputype; PE `MZ` + `PE\0\0` + machine
0x8664), a missing source / failed compile / empty output / wrong arch
failing by name. Named exclusions: cybs (no Mach-O / PE form), cycc and
cyrius (the builders' own lines), cyrius-lsp on Windows (cycc_win refuses
it). macOS newly ships cyrius-lsp, ark, cyrld, ts_test_runner and
cyrsign-efi, and its `bin/cyriusly` is the compiled version manager (the
shell twin stays at scripts/cyriusly); Windows newly ships ark, cyrld,
cyriusly, ts_test_runner and cyrsign-efi `.exe`, `cyriusly.exe`'s POSIX
verbs refusing by name (6.7.16's D84 — cyriusly_native_port_v51110).
Through 6.7.15 the three builders compiled a hand list. The basis is
6.7.14's D59 (the aarch64 tarball) extended to these builders — beyond
D59's text, filed for confirmation; D84 decided only the Windows
`cyriusly.exe`.

Tarballs uploaded to the GitHub Release page.

== `install.sh` ==

Curl-pipeable installer:
    curl -sSf https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MacCracken/cyrius/main/scripts/install.sh | sh

Detects host triple, downloads the matching tarball, extracts to
`~/.cyrius/<version>/`, symlinks `~/.cyrius/bin/*` to the latest
version. `cyriusly setup` runs after to put `~/.cyrius/bin` on
PATH and write the `.cyriusrc` profile snippet.

== `cbt/pulsar.cyr` ==

Internal toolchain build orchestrator. Invoked by `cyrius
pulsar` (mostly a maintainer-side verb). Cross-compiles
`build/cycc-native-aarch64` so users on ARM64 hardware can
self-host without a manual cross-compile pass.

The `cycc-native-aarch64` binary is committed to `build/`
(gitignored except this one + the prior-major seed; see CLAUDE.md
"build/ tracking policy").

[[entries]]
name = "audit_test_check_pipeline"
description = "scripts/check.sh + scripts/release-gate.sh + cyrius audit — the full-validation pipeline (240 gates at v6.6.1)"
category = "cyrius"
---
The validation pipeline runs in three layers, with a separate
consolidated PRE-TAG gate on top.

== LAYER 1 — `scripts/check.sh` (CI-grade, 240 gates / 0 failed at v6.6.1) ==

The repo's primary acceptance gate. Runs end-to-end and reports
N/M with each gate as PASS / FAIL / SKIP. The count grows every minor
— re-derive it, never quote a remembered number (this entry sat at
"64 gates at v5.8.19" for a year). Categories:

  - Build verifies: cycc compiles, cycc_aarch64 cross compiles,
    cycc_cx bytecode roundtrip, Mach-O cross, PE cross
  - Self-host verifies: byte-identical two-step + three-step
  - Heap map: 100 regions / 0 overlaps at v6.5.10, structure intact
  - Test suite: every .tcyr under tests/tcyr/<bucket>/, read
    recursively (482 files at 6.6.19; 260, then flat, at v6.5.10)
  - Format: cyrfmt --check on stdlib + src
  - Lint: cyrlint on stdlib + src (cyrlint has no --check; since 6.6.5 an unknown flag is refused by name)
  - api-surface: snapshot drift detection (5152 public fns at v6.6.1)
  - Capacity: each cap (frame, heap, code, fixup) within budget
  - Topic-grouped TS suite: lex_combined / parse_core /
    parse_decls / parse_advanced
  - Per-feature shell gates under `tests/gates/<bucket>/*.sh` (8
    buckets since v6.5.11) — **372 at 6.6.19, ALL of them live**:
    some registered by exact path in `programs/checks/main.cyr`, the
    rest driven from `scripts/check.sh`. DERIVE with
    `find tests/gates -name '*.sh' | wc -l`; a flat `tests/*.sh` glob
    matches nothing since v6.5.11. (v6.5.10 history: 41 files then
    flat in `tests/*.sh`, 31 by exact path, 10 via named gate fns such
    as `_heapmap_gate`.) ⚠ Docs in several repos have claimed
    "10" or "19" here — that number was wrong and kept being
    incremented rather than re-derived. NONE of these are named
    `regression-*.sh`: the .sh-conversion arc closed at v5.9.41 with
    zero such files remaining, and the driver is
    `programs/checks/*.cyr` (sovereign cyrius), with
    `scripts/check.sh` a thin shim.

⚠ check.sh's grep summary can mask a `.tcyr` segfault / exit-code
failure. Run a per-file exit-code loop before claiming green.

At the 6.7.15 gate (2026-10-19, `dc6533f90`): 131 / 131 driver rows and
569 / 569 shell gates (the 2 named agnos-parity SKIPs) — a dated figure;
re-derive it. At the 6.7.16 wave-4 merge: 582 / 582 shell gates, 0 SKIPs
(578 gate scripts; the 6.7.16 release gate's own counts are in CHANGELOG
[6.7.16]).

A LANGUAGE DOES NOT GATE ON OTHER PROJECTS (6.7.16, W0-1; the user,
2026-10-19). The gates that read a consumer's checkout left — agnos's four
ABI / kernel parity gates, the mirshi runtime axes, the check driver's sit
fixture row (sankoch's DEFLATE / zlib is tested in-tree instead), the
secureyeoman TypeScript corpus rows, agnoshi's crossbuild part 2 — and
`removed_symbol_census.sh` reads this checkout only (every removed public
name needs its docs/retired-symbols.allow row). `RG_SKIP_ALLOW` is EMPTY:
the release gate passes no SKIP (its two entries existed only for the mirshi
axes), so every package dev-tools-linux.md lists is required on a box that
gates releases. The acceptance gate, tests/gates/toolchain/
gates_read_only_the_tree.sh, refuses on every non-comment line of
tests/gates/**/*.sh, the top-level scripts/*.sh and programs/checks/*.cyr a
`$HOME/Repos` read in any spelling, a literal `/home/<user>/` path,
`CYRIUS_SIBLING_ROOT`, `<root>/..` or `dirname "<root>"` — an allowlist of
zero.

check.sh's REAPERS: `_chk_reap_stale_homes` (`cyrius-check-home.*`),
`_chk_reap_dead_cli_tmpdirs` (dead-pid `cyrius-<pid>` dirs) and, since
6.7.16 (LF-4), `_chk_reap_dead_capture_files` — under the capture verbs' own
base (an absolute TMPDIR, else `/tmp`), only `audit_x[ca]_<digits>[.<digits>]`,
only `-f`, `! -L`, `-O` files older than `CYRIUS_CHECK_REAP_MINS` (default
240; 0 turns the age gate off) whose pid is dead, one line naming what it
reaped (gate: check_stale_home_reaper.sh axes 17 – 22).

THE CHECK DRIVER NO LONGER DIES OF SIGPIPE (6.7.15, W0-1). The driver
pipes fixtures into binaries through `regression_pipe_write_all`
(lib/regression.cyr). A child that exits WITHOUT reading its stdin
closes the pipe's read end, and the next write raised SIGPIPE, whose
default action killed the CALLER: the driver died with 141 in
`_monomorph_substrate_gate` (an 847-byte fixture piped into a binary
that exits 42 unread), and every driver row after it was left without a
verdict. Since 6.7.15 the write runs with SIGPIPE ignored when its
disposition is SIG_DFL and puts it back after (the write then answers
EPIPE, a short count); a caller's own ignore or handler stands, and a
child forked before the write keeps the caller's disposition. Row:
check_driver_bounded.sh axis 2e.

THE FMT / LINT READ-CAP HEADROOM ROW (6.7.15, W0-2 — 6.7.15's D25 A).
cyrfmt and cyrlint read at most `_MAX_FILE` bytes of a file (1,052,672 B,
1028 KB since 6.2.20); a file past it cannot be checked at all.
`src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr` had reached 1,049,325 B (6.7.14 alone grew
it 100 KB), so 6.7.15 split it — the top-level scans, the definition
identity / duplicate checks and `_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL` moved unchanged to
`src/frontend/parse_fn_def.cyr`, included right after it (the token
stream is the same: every compiler byte-identical, seed-derive OK) — and
`tests/gates/toolchain/tracked_sources_canonically_formatted.sh` gained
a headroom row: every swept `.cyr` OUTSIDE lib/ stays 64 KB under the
cap, read from both tools' sources, which must agree. lib/ is outside
the row: its large files are the vendored folds (sigil, mabda, sankoch
are past the cap already), whose layout is their source repo's. When
the row names a file, split it. (6.7.18 moved the whole parser into
folders, each piece unchanged: parse_fn.cyr is src/frontend/parse/fn/*,
parse_fn_def.cyr is parse/fn/def.cyr.)

THE OPT-IN MODES' CORPUS LEG (6.7.15, IT-2): `tests/gates/ir-opt/
ir3_dce_corpus.sh` (`# check: serial`) compiles and runs every
`tests/tcyr/**/*.tcyr` under `CYRIUS_IR=3` + `CYRIUS_DCE=1` together —
one leg per mode PAIR, because a defect only the pair acts on passed
every per-mode leg — with an expected-failure list that may only shrink
(empty) and the corpus floor. Its anti-vacuous plant is a shim COMPILER
(no predefine shows `CYRIUS_IR` / `CYRIUS_DCE` to a source). See
compiler_env_vars § IR PIPELINE.

GATES UNDER `bash -eo pipefail` (6.7.15 — PL-1 – PL-4, FE-2, IT-10,
IT-9r). check.sh and the driver run each shell gate with `sh`; GitHub's
`run:` shell is `bash -eo pipefail`, where six gates stopped with a
non-zero rc and NO verdict line (syscall_xlat_generated,
removed_symbol_census, stdlib_modules_self_sufficient,
default_named_args_checked, lsp_diagnostics_file_column and the new
dce_seed_pass_linear), and two counted wrong silently on the way. The
shapes: `n=$(grep -c …)` (grep prints 0 AND exits 1 on the passing
case); `git tag … | head -1` and `cmd | grep -q` / `| head -N` (the
writer dies of SIGPIPE once its output passes the 64 KB pipe buffer,
and pipefail makes the pipeline fail — so an `if … | grep -q` reads
FALSE on a hit: `removed_symbol_census.sh` counted 297 files where `sh`
counts 380, and `stdlib_modules_self_sufficient.sh` took
`lib/sankoch.cyr` for a first-party module); `( … ); x=$?` on a probe
that exits non-zero by design; `trap '' PIPE` making a late write EPIPE,
which `-e` then ends on; a best-of-N loop ending `[ … ] && WX=$e`. The
fixes: count with `grep -c … || true` and test the count, ask git for
one line (`git for-each-ref --count=1 --sort=-v:refname …`), capture as
`x=0; cmd || x=$?`, let a write take its failure (`printf … || true`),
write a minimum update as `if`. The meta-row
`tests/gates/toolchain/gates_verdict_under_pipefail.sh` runs the
listed gates under `bash -eo pipefail` and requires a PASS (or named
SKIP) last line, with a self-test that a zero-count `grep -c` and a
SIGPIPE'd `yes | head -1` read as STOPPED; the lsp gate re-runs itself
under it, and the DCE-seed gate's row P0 runs its helpers in a child
under it. ⚠ Inside a gate, `-e` is OFF in any `||` /
`&&` / `if` / `!` context and everything it calls, so "does this survive
`-e`" needs a child `bash -eo pipefail -c` (lsp row R0, DCE row P0).

== THE GATE SKIP PROTOCOL — EXIT 77 (cycc 6.6.11) ==

A shell gate that COULD NOT RUN its check (missing tool, host, fixture,
/proc, wine, …) prints its SKIP line and exits **77** (automake's SKIP).
Exit 0 means it ran and passed; anything else is a FAIL; 124 is the
supervisor's deadline alone. Both runners score 77 the same way — the
check driver (`_gate_score`, programs/checks/main.cyr) and
`scripts/check.sh` (`_chk_gate`): a SKIP row with its own count, never a
pass. A run with skips ends `GREEN, with N gate(s) SKIPPED — no failure,
but not everything was checked` and lists them (exit stays 0), instead
of ALL GREEN.

`CYRIUS_CHECK_NO_SKIP=1` turns every SKIP into a FAIL (`SKIP REFUSED`).
CI runs the driver's delegated rows under it, so a CI runner missing a
tool goes red instead of green. The variable is read by BOTH runners
with one contract: `1` = strict, unset / empty / `0` = report skips,
anything else (`=yes`, `=true`) is REFUSED with exit 2 before anything
runs — a strict mode that silently read "off" would be a no-op.

⛔ WHY: until 6.6.11 the shell-gate protocol was binary. A gate that
could not run had only `echo "SKIP: …"; exit 0`, and both runners
scored that as a PASS — the SKIP line was prose nobody graded, and
`CYRIUS_CHECK_NO_SKIP` (added for the driver's own rows at 6.6.9) never
reached a shell gate. The census in
tests/gates/toolchain/check_driver_skip_is_not_pass.sh (axis 11) now
holds every script under tests/gates to it: none may print SKIP and
then exit 0 (a planted `{ echo "SKIP: …"; exit 0; }` is reported by
file:line). When you write a gate, a whole-gate skip is `exit 77`.

== LAYER 2 — `cyrius audit` (dev-loop driver) ==

Default (`cyrius audit`, mode 0) is the PROJECT sweep — fmt / lint /
docs / tests / bench over the current project, works in any repo and
needs no `scripts/check.sh`. `--internal` runs the cyrius-internal
check.sh; `--internal=platform-check` adds the cross-OS real-hardware
self-host. Since v6.5.8 any other argument is an ERROR rather than
being silently absorbed. Its FMT stage runs `cyrfmt --check` and takes
ITS verdict (6.7.12, D26 — TL-20): exit 1 with the "not canonically
formatted" line = needs reformatting; any other failure is an error
naming the file. Until 6.7.12 audit byte-compared `cyrfmt <file>`'s
rewrite with the file and flagged files `--check` passed. The contract:
canonical 2-space continuation, 4 accepted, deeper rejected; `cyrius
fmt` rewrites an accepted 4 as 2. To require the canonical form exactly,
compare `cyrfmt <file>` with the file yourself.

== LAYER 3 — `cyrius soak [N]` (long-running) ==

Wraps check.sh in N iterations + walks tests/scyr/*.scyr.
Catches non-determinism and codegen drift across runs.

== THE PRE-TAG GATE — `sh scripts/release-gate.sh` ==

The single consolidated check that must be GREEN before EVERY `.NN`
tag. Five fail-fast steps: (1) self-host fixpoint, (2) **seed derive**
`seed → cybs → cycc` byte-identical, (3) check.sh, (4) cross-OS
self-host on REAL hardware, (5) bench (non-blocking, recorded in the
CHANGELOG). It exists so no individual gate gets run à la carte and
skipped.

⚠ Step 2 is NOT covered by step 1. `cybs` — the hand-assembly
bootstrap compiler the 29 KB seed assembles — is far more limited than
`build/cycc` and used to fail SILENTLY on things `build/cycc` compiles
fine. Since 6.7.11 it says so: a construct it does not support or any
syntax error prints `cybs: syntax error, or a construct the bootstrap
compiler does not support (const, loop, do/while, an if-expression,
unary minus, x[i], >>>, >>=), at: <the line of the expanded stream>` (`x[i]`
listed since 6.7.14; grep
the line to find the `src/` file), an unknown name `cybs: undefined
variable or function (cybs has no const): <name>, at: <line>`, and a
statement call to a never-defined fn `cybs: undefined function:
<name>` (it compiled to a crashing binary). cybs compiles fn bodies
BEFORE top-level statements, so a const a fn reads is reported at the
READER's line. Since 6.7.14 (IN-10, IN-11) an input that ends right
after `fn` / `var` / `var x:` is refused with the class message at the
keyword's line (pass 0 stepped past the EOF token: SIGSEGV, no message),
and an undefined name before `[` (`zz[1] = 5;`) is named as the read
form names it — only before `[`, because `loop`, `const` and `do` lex as
NAMES in cybs and must keep the class message; and (IN-12)
cybs's PREPROCESSOR caps are refused by name — 15 MB stdin, a 2 MB slot
per include depth, 8 depths, a 16 MB expansion (each was unchecked: an
over-2 MB include had its tail overwritten by the file it includes,
stdin past 15 MB was dropped at exit 0). `src/main.cyr` expands to
~4.4 MB, its largest file ~0.95 MB, 3 deep; row S2 watches it with an
awk walker that must EQUAL cybs's own expansion length. Since 6.7.15
(IT-4) its INCLUDE-ONCE LIST is refused past too — `cybs: more than 256
distinct includes, the size of the preprocessor's include-once list` and
`cybs: an include path of 256 or more characters, the width of an
include-once entry` (rc 1, nothing written); a re-include is still
skipped first, a 255-character spelling fits, and the list's stores are
byte stores. Through 6.7.14 the list (256 entries of 256 B) was
unchecked: the 257th distinct include marked itself onto depth 0's name
buffer and the next include was skipped as seen (exit 0 where the
source said 42), a 256+ character path ran into the next entry so its
re-include ran the file twice, and a qword store wrote 7 bytes past the
last entry's NUL, so the 256th distinct include with a 249 – 255
character path was skipped silently. src/main.cyr's closure is 31
includes of ~40 characters, so nothing cybs compiles changed. And
(IT-3) a top-level `zz[1] = 5;` after a fn whose LOCAL is zz is named
(`cybs: undefined variable or function (cybs has no const): zz, at:
zz[1] = 5;`): `parse_fn_def` left the fn-local count standing, so the
`[` lookup found the last fn's locals at the top level (the limit
6.7.14's IN-11 recorded). Still silent: the per-fn reference ceiling
and the seed's caps — all three (input, labels, code) guarded for
`bootstrap/cybs.cyr` by gate row S of `cybs_call_arity_named.sh` since
6.7.11 (6.7.15: input 120,567 / 131,072 B, labels 508 / 512, code
23,183 / 65,536 B; 6.7.14: 119,157, 508, 22,890; 6.7.11: 115,974, 506,
22,200). Mandatory for ANY `src/` change, on EVERY release.

Step 4 runs FOUR hosts — ecb (macOS-arm64) · ach (Intel-Mac
x86-macho) · cass (Windows PE) · pi (aarch64) — each reporting
`SELFHOST_OK` + `LIBTEST_OK`; the legs run CONCURRENTLY beside check.sh
(`CYRIUS_GATE_SERIAL=1` for the old sequential walk). A green CI
checkmark is NOT this. ⚠ SINCE 6.7.12 (D8, the user 2026-10-15 — WN-2) the
hosts in `scripts/cross-os-selfhost.sh`'s `CO_FULL_HOSTS` run the WHOLE
`tests/tcyr` corpus by default when the leg runs a lib-test. Since 6.7.14
(PL-4) `CO_FULL_HOSTS` is `ecb ach pi cass` — all four: cass ran 627 / 627
once the PE read / open defects were fixed (PL-1 – PL-3), so every host runs
the whole corpus at step 4 (through 6.7.13 cass ran the
`tests/tcyr/crossos/` directory only). `CYRIUS_CROSS_OS_FULL=0`
forces the crossos subset, and `CYRIUS_CROSS_OS_FULL=1` alone STARTS the
lib-test (it needed an arg 2 before 6.7.12, so the documented full-corpus
command ran nothing). A run with 0 assertions is graded a FAILURE — a test
whose every arm can SKIP must assert that an arm ran. The
directory-is-the-selector rule still holds for a NEW syscall wrapper: its
companion goes in `crossos/`, the cross-OS selector (the subset every
host runs with `CYRIUS_CROSS_OS_FULL=0`) — a wrapper with no file there
was never RUN on Windows while cass ran the subset, which is exactly how six
syscall-wrapper defects survived until v6.5.7 added
`tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_wrappers.tcyr`.
⛔ **This paragraph said "only the `vr01_` glob".** That FILENAME PREFIX was
retired at v6.5.11 and zero such files remain, so following this text — naming a
new test `vr01_*` to get it cross-tested — achieves the exact opposite and opts
the test OUT. The selector is the directory.

== CLOSEOUT-PASS DISCIPLINE ==

CLAUDE.md "Closeout Pass (before every minor/major bump)" defines a
12-step protocol (plus step 3b) that runs at the end of each minor
cycle. Mechanical (1-3, 3b) → judgment-call (4-8) → compliance (9-10)
→ docs (11 CHANGELOG/roadmap/vidya sync, 12 backlog re-triage).
Self-host break short-circuits the chain.

[[entries]]
name = "minimum_compiler_version_pin"
description = "`cyrius` field in cyrius.cyml — pin a minimum compiler version, error early if older"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius.cyml` supports a top-level `cyrius` field specifying the
minimum compiler version a project requires. `cyrius build`
checks this and errors early if the installed toolchain is older
than declared.

USAGE:

    cyrius = "5.8.27"        # minimum compiler version

PATTERN: same shape as Rust's `rust-version`, Go's `go`
directive in go.mod, Zig's `minimum_zig_version` in build.zig.zon.

WHEN TO BUMP YOUR PROJECT'S MINIMUM:
  - Using a new language feature (slices, multi-width types,
    dot-syntax field access on Str / slice locals, etc.)
  - Using a new stdlib module that wasn't in earlier releases
    (e.g. `lib/random.cyr`, `lib/security.cyr` from v5.7.35;
    `lib/test.cyr` from v5.7.43; slice-typed wrappers from v5.8.18)
  - Using a new compiler directive (`#ifplat`, `#regalloc`,
    `#must_use`, `#derive`)
  - Hitting a bug fix in a specific patch release (e.g.
    16-byte fn-local layout-flip needed v5.8.16+)

`${file:VERSION}` interpolation works in `cyrius.cyml`, so a
project with its own VERSION file can avoid duplicating the
literal — vidya itself uses this pattern.

The minimum-version check fires at `cyrius build` time before
parse, so the error message is clear ("requires cyrius >= X.Y.Z;
have Y.X.Z") instead of failing later with a syntax error on
something the older parser doesn't understand.

[[entries]]
name = "cyriusly_native_port_v51110"
description = "cyriusly — toolchain-version management CLI, native cyrius port of scripts/cyriusly at v5.11.10"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyriusly` is the user-facing CLI for switching between
installed cyrius toolchain versions. v5.11.10 closed the
shell-script → cyrius native port — `programs/cyriusly.cyr`
compiles to a binary in `[release].bins`. The bash script
`scripts/cyriusly` remains as a backend for `setup` /
`cmdtools` verbs that shell out to OS tools.

== INSTALL / UNINSTALL / UPDATE ==

  cyriusly install <version>     — fetch + extract binary
                                   release to ~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/
  cyriusly uninstall <version>   — remove ~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/
  cyriusly update                — check GitHub for newer version
                                   (curl GitHub API; no telemetry).
                                   Since 6.7.16 (TT-17) it compares the
                                   active version of the install it
                                   manages — `CYRIUS_HOME` honoured, as
                                   `version` does; it read
                                   `~/.cyrius/current` and offered "Update
                                   available: none -> <latest>" under
                                   `CYRIUS_HOME`. Its script is a
                                   constant run with the active version as
                                   argv `$1`.

== VERSION SWITCH ==

  cyriusly use <version>         — PIN this project's cyrius.cyml
                                   ([package].cyrius) to <version> — the
                                   compiled cyriusly's meaning since
                                   v5.11.26 (this row read "set
                                   ~/.cyrius/current" until 6.7.16)
  cyriusly use <version> --global — switch the active version
                                   (~/.cyrius/current → <version>; the
                                   shell twin's plain `use`). Since 6.7.16
                                   (TT-16) the compiled binary's own hints
                                   say so: `Cannot uninstall active
                                   version. Switch first: cyriusly use
                                   <other> --global`, `update`'s `Run:
                                   cyriusly install <v> && cyriusly use
                                   <v> --global`, and help lists both
                                   forms (they were the shell twin's
                                   hints, which switched nothing here)
  cyriusly use                   — print the resolved version + source
                                   (cyml-pinned / global / latest fallback)

== INFO ==

  cyriusly version               — print active toolchain version
  cyriusly list                  — list installed versions
  cyriusly ls                    — alias for list
  cyriusly which                 — print active binary path
  cyriusly home                  — print CYRIUS_HOME (~/.cyrius/)
  cyriusly help                  — verb list

== SETUP / CMDTOOLS ==

  cyriusly setup                 — shell out to scripts/cyriusly (
                                   shell aliases + completions install)
                                   Since 6.7.12 (TL-4) the compiled
                                   setup REFUSES without build/cycc
                                   (bootstrap.sh builds cybs and asm,
                                   never a cycc — restore it with `git
                                   checkout build/cycc`), has no cross-bin
                                   loop (install.sh --refresh-only builds
                                   them), and builds / installs with the
                                   four compile-mode switches
                                   (CYRIUS_BOUNDS / POISON / COVERAGE /
                                   COVERAGE_FILE) unset. Windows: refused
                                   by name (6.7.16 — below).
  cyriusly cmdtools              — shell out to scripts/cyriusly (
                                   develop-mode helper bins). Windows:
                                   refused by name (6.7.16 — below).

== WINDOWS (6.7.16 — the user's D84; TT-12, TT-12r, TT-14) ==

The Windows tarball ships `cyriusly.exe` since 6.7.16. `install`, `update`,
`setup`, `uninstall`, `cmdtools` and `use --global` (with or without a
version) refuse by name — `cyriusly: <verb> is not available on Windows:
<why>` and what to do instead (the `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File
install.ps1 -Tarball cyrius-<v>-x86_64-windows.tar.gz` command; `uninstall`:
`rmdir /s /q "<home>\versions\<v>"`), rc 1, before any output or change; a
malformed operand, the active-version guard and `uninstall`'s not-installed
answer ("Version <v> not installed.", rc 0) still come first (⚠ beyond D84's
text). `list`, `version`, `which`, `home`, `use <v>` (the project pin),
`--help` and `--version` work, and every hint they print names install.ps1
(⚠ beyond D84's text). The home is `CYRIUS_HOME`, else `%USERPROFILE%\.cyrius`
(install.ps1's default, the CLI's) — `HOME` is not read first on Windows (⚠ it
moves an ssh session's home); `which` prints `<home>\bin\cycc.exe`. Before
TT-12 (inside 6.7.16, once the tools lane shipped the binary) `install
6.7.15` printed "Installing Cyrius 6.7.15..." and exited -1 with no reason,
and `use --global` left a `bin.cyrtmp.<pid>.<n>` link behind. Gate:
win_cyriusly_posix_verbs_refused.sh; the cass leg.

== INTERACTION WITH cyrius.cyml ==

Pre-v5.11.17, `cyrius deps` resolved stdlib_dir from the global
~/.cyrius/lib symlink — meaning `cyriusly use <other>` from a
sibling agent could wipe in-flight lib/ edits via the snapshot-
ping-pong loop. v5.11.17 fix: deps resolution prefers ./lib (for
the cyrius source repo) then `~/.cyrius/versions/<cyml-pinned>/
lib` (for downstream consumers) before the legacy global. See
`cyrius_deps_global_symlink_wedge_v51117` field note.

== COMPILED BINARY ==

`programs/cyriusly.cyr` → `~/.cyrius/bin/cyriusly` (~90 KB).
Heavy verbs (install / update / uninstall / setup / cmdtools)
use `exec_cmd` to shell out where the operation is fundamentally
shell-shaped (tarball extraction, curl downloads). Light verbs
(version / list / which / home / help / use) are full native.

[[entries]]
name = "manifest_key_vocabulary_and_precedence_v6617"
description = "Since cyrius 6.6.17 cyrius.cyml is the build tool's configuration: one declared key vocabulary (`cyrius help manifest`), one reader of the whole manifest as TOML, one precedence rule for every key — argument > environment > manifest > default — and `cyrius build --print-config` to see each value with its origin. `[build] test`, `dce` and `defines` are read; `strict` and `target` are held; `features` is dropped"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrius.cyml", "manifest", "build", "precedence", "print-config", "toml", "v6.6.17"]
---
== SEE WHAT A BUILD WILL USE ==

    cyrius build --print-config [<source> [<output>]]      # abridged sketch of its output:
      build.entry  = "src/main.cyr"    (manifest: [build] src)    <- names the synonym read
      build.output = "build/app"       (argument: <output>)
      build.dce    = true              (environment: CYRIUS_DCE)
      build.strict = ...               [held: no effect since 6.3.2]

It exits 0 BEFORE the dep resolver runs, so it writes neither `lib/` nor `build/`.

== PRECEDENCE — the same for every key, no exceptions ==

    argument  >  environment  >  manifest  >  default

A key with no argument or environment channel just lacks that rung.

== THE [build] KEYS ==

    entry / src   read     the source (`src` is a declared synonym of `entry`)
    output        read     a DEFAULT, not an assertion; `cyrius init --bin` writes build/{PROJ}
    test          read     bare `cyrius test` runs it (a file, a dir of .tcyr, or a list) FIRST,
                           then the tests/ walk — each file once; a missing path is a named failure.
                           6.7.6: `[test] files` is the spelling, this its synonym (both: warned)
    test_standalone read   6.7.6: each test compiles with only its own includes (no stdlib prepend)
    dce           read     --dce / CYRIUS_DCE=1|0 (empty = unset; anything else refused) / dce = true
    defines       read     -D NAME / CYRIUS_DEFINES=A,B / defines = ["A", "B"] — every `cyrius build`
                           in the project; -D on test / run / bench stays a command-line choice
    strict        HELD     warned, not read: `cycc --strict` has had no effect since 6.3.2
    target        HELD     warned, not read (recognised; use the CLI's target flags)
    features      DROPPED  warned by name; nothing ever read it

`[lsp] skip` (6.7.16, TL-13) is read by cyrius-lsp: the files it never compiles, paths from the project root
(`--print-config` row `lsp.skip`; an unknown `[lsp]` key warned, a value that is no path or array of paths refused —
cyrius_cyml_manifest).
An unknown `[build]` key (a typo) is warned by name; `[coverage] programs` is read by
`cyrius coverage` (TEXT coverage, in both modes), `[coverage] exec` (6.7.10) by `cyrius coverage`
too — a list of program globs built instrumented and run; it alone turns `--exec` on. `cyrius help manifest` prints the whole vocabulary (every section, each key's
status, synonyms, channels and reader), and the guide's "Manifest keys" table is gated equal to it.

== THE READER ==

The whole file (refused by name past 16 MiB), the CYML prose body cut off, values as TOML:
`"…"` with escapes and `\uXXXX`, `'…'` verbatim, `true` / `false`, arrays with comments. Keys
compare WHOLE (`dev-stdlib` is never `stdlib`). Before 6.6.17 every key family had its own capped
scanner (a `[build]` past 32,767 B was invisible) and its own value rule (`entry = '…'` read as
absent; `path = '../foo'` took the next double-quoted string in the file).

== WHY A GATE MUST NOT SHARE THE CODE'S SPELLING ==

v6.5.49's `[build]` fallback read `src` while 120 of 125 manifests wrote `entry`, and its gate
passed because its fixture used `src` too. 6.6.17's inventory gate takes its expected keys from
what consumers WRITE (an ecosystem census, the init templates, package-format.md). See
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml `check_which_manifest_key_CONSUMERS_write_not_the_one_your_repo_writes`.

CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (Manifest — P1).

[[entries]]
name = "coverage_run_programs_v6617"
description = "Since cyrius 6.6.17 `cyrius coverage` takes RUN programs as a corpus — `[coverage] programs = [\"programs/*_test.cyr\"]` in cyrius.cyml or `--programs <glob>` (repeatable; it replaces the manifest list) — and `--per-entry` lists which public fns each corpus entry references. It is TEXT coverage (references), not execution coverage"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["coverage", "cyrius.cyml", "run-programs", "per-entry", "P5", "v6.6.17"]
---
    [coverage]
    programs = ["programs/*_test.cyr"]

    cyrius coverage                          # tests/**/*.tcyr + the declared programs
    cyrius coverage --programs 'bench/*.cyr' # replaces the manifest list (argument > manifest)
    cyrius coverage --per-entry              # per corpus entry: the public fns it references

- each matched program is counted exactly as a `.tcyr` is: whole identifiers in code, with
  comments and strings blanked;
- `*` / `?` stay inside one path segment (`programs/*_test.cyr` does not reach `programs/sub/`);
- a glob that matches nothing, or a program that does not exist, is a named failure — a typo must
  not silently drop the corpus;
- a corpus program is a test, never measured surface (a file that DEFINES a fn would otherwise
  "reference" it by declaring it).

⚠ TEXT coverage, the reference count `.tcyr` corpora get since 6.6.8 — not execution coverage.
Instrumented-build-and-run coverage (P5-B) shipped in 6.7.10: `cyrius coverage --exec` /
`[coverage] exec` — see execution_coverage_mode_v6710. `[coverage] programs` stays TEXT coverage
even under --exec.
CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (Coverage — P5-A).

[[entries]]
name = "lib_sync_relock_v6617"
description = "Since cyrius 6.6.17 `cyrius lib sync` (scoped and `--full`) re-locks the files it writes in cyrius.lock and is held to the same stdlib-leaf guard as `cyrius deps`: under an unchanged pin a lib/ file whose locked hash differs from the snapshot is refused by name and nothing is written. `--relock` accepts a moved snapshot explicitly. After a pin move, run `lib sync` BEFORE `deps` / `build`"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lib-sync", "cyrius.lock", "deps", "pin-move", "relock", "v6.6.17"]
---
== THE PIN-MOVE RECIPE ==

    # edit [package] cyrius = "6.6.17"
    cyrius lib sync --full        # re-vendors the new snapshot AND re-locks what it wrote
    cyrius deps --verify          # passes

What `lib sync` does now: plan every (snapshot file, lib/ destination) pair; run the 6.6.4 guard
over every destination (a pin change re-locks; an unchanged pin with a mismatching locked hash is
refused and NOTHING is written; `--dry-run` refuses too); then re-hash only the rows for files it
wrote, drop rows for files gone from disk, keep every other row byte for byte (so a local edit to
a file it did not write still fails `deps --verify`), and carry commit pins forward. A project
with no lock gets none from `lib sync` — the first lock still comes from `cyrius deps`.

⚠ ORDER (6.6.17 – 6.7.11): `cyrius deps` / `build` run after the pin edit but BEFORE `lib sync`
stamped the new pin over lock rows for files deps does not vendor; those rows still held the
previous pin's bytes and looked exactly like "the snapshot moved under an unchanged pin", and
`lib sync` then refused them by name.
SINCE 6.7.12 (D19 — TL-12 / TL-12r): `cyrius deps` / `cyrius build` at a moved pin REFUSE to re-lock
when lib/ holds an out-of-closure file the new snapshot ships with other bytes: rc 1, cyrius.lock
UNCHANGED, each file named, remedies `cyrius lib sync --full` or `rm <file> && cyrius deps`;
`--relock` does not bypass it. The recipe (`lib sync --full` first) is unchanged.
SINCE 6.7.13 (the user's D41, D42; TL-10, TL-11, TL-12): (1) a REFUSED `deps` / `build` WRITES NOTHING
— lib/ AND cyrius.lock byte-unchanged, `--verify` green — because at such a bump the resolve first
runs once, silently, into a private scratch to learn the closure (D41; through 6.7.12 the closure
files were already at the new pin after the refusal and `--verify` failed on them). (2) At a moved
pin a bare `deps --lock` (and `publish`'s lock step) REFUSES while any lib/ file differs from the new
snapshot — its ways out are `cyrius deps` and `lib sync --full`; a SCOPED `lib sync` / `update`
refuses before copying while an out-of-subset file differs — its way out is `--full`; `--no-lock` is
exempt (D42). 6.7.12's D19 rule now reads: EVERY lock writer. (3) `deps --locked` at a moved pin
names the same files and ways (TL-10; it said "run `cyrius deps`"). `lib sync`'s refusal summary names
`cyrius lib sync --relock` first, `--full --relock` for a whole-snapshot project. If the pin was
NOT moved, the installed snapshot changed under it — reinstall that version; do not `--relock`.
`cyrius update` (D18, 6.7.12) writes what `lib sync` writes — see
cyrius_cyml_git_first_local_switch_v676.

Before 6.6.17 `lib sync` never touched the lock, so every pin move ended in `deps --verify`
failures until the lock was regenerated from empty (kriya 1.7.3: 72 verified, 39 failed).
CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (t1).

[[entries]]
name = "distlib_sidecar_is_what_the_compile_verify_proves_v6618"
description = "Since cyrius 6.6.18 (P4 option 2) a fold's `dist/<pkg>.deps` sidecar is derived ONLY by the compile-verify fixpoint, over six targets (x86_64 Linux / Windows / macOS / agnos, aarch64 Linux / macOS). The producer's `[deps] stdlib`, its umbrella includes and its test-only leaves (`assert`, `bench`) no longer reach it. Every bundle carries the same leaves as a compile-verified requires block, so `include \"dist/<pkg>.cyr\"` alone compiles. A consumer whose build fails on a leaf it never declared gets a `hint:` naming the leaf"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["distlib", "deps-sidecar", "requires-block", "compile-verify", "p4", "option-2", "undefined-leaf-hint", "migration", "v6.6.18"]
---
== PRODUCER, AT THE 6.6.18 PIN BUMP ==

    cyrius distlib --check     # STALE: "the committed bundle predates cyrius 6.6.18's requires block — run cyrius distlib --all"
    cyrius distlib --all       # regenerates every bundle + sidecar; commit dist/

Expect the sidecar to SHRINK (bayan 13→10, ganita 10→3, yukti 18→3 …) and the bundle to gain

    # Requires (compile-verified; the leaves of dist/<pkg>.deps):
    include "lib/<leaf>.cyr"      # one per sidecar leaf, same order, families expanded

`[deps] stdlib` still drives auto-prepend and `cyrius deps` for the package's OWN builds. A name
the converged verify can attribute to nothing is printed as a warning with its targets (a
deliberate consumer hook shows up there); a non-symbol failure on agnos ALONE is a warning too.

== CONSUMER ==

A leaf you used only because some producer's sidecar OVER-reported it disappears when that
producer regenerates. Since 6.6.19 every VENDORED fold in lib/ carries its compile-verified
requires block, so `include "lib/sandhi.cyr"` alone compiles (re-verified 6.6.20). For a fold
that has not regenerated, or a non-vendored bundle, put every leaf your build needs into your
own `[deps] stdlib`. The CLI names it under the error:

    hint: 'sakshi_span_enter' is defined by stdlib leaf 'sakshi' — add it to [deps] stdlib in cyrius.cyml

Add it, `cyrius deps`, rebuild; repeat while a `hint:` names another. A family (`lib/unicode/`) is
one leaf named by the family and expands from the PRODUCER's snapshot, so a consumer pinned to a
cyrius lacking a member gets a named `cannot open include file` — bump the pin.

Why: the v6.5.10 union published every producer's test leaves to every consumer (75 sidecars named
`assert`, 68 of those bundles used none of it) and hid real gaps behind a declaration. cyrius
`docs/development/ecosystem-migration-6.6.18.md`; CHANGELOG [6.6.18] (Distlib — P4 option 2).

6.7.11 (TL-4): the owner of an undefined name is chosen by ONE rule on every target or some (the every-target path
used to be the partial rule minus its in-unit skip and its tie refusal); a tie between two non-peer stdlib leaves is
REFUSED by name with no sidecar written — include the right leaf from the module that uses it. The census beside the
rule (`distlib_owner_rule_shared.sh` row C) allowlists the filed ties (none since 6.7.12: the `WS_*` tie was the last,
closed by D11) and goes RED on a new one AND on a stale row; at the 6.7.11 merge it caught `_bre_err` (niyama / sankoch, fixed upstream in sankoch 2.8.7). See compiler_flags
§ `cyrius distlib` FLAGS.

6.7.14 (LIB-9): BOTH distlib checks — the sidecar's compile-verify and the bundle self-check — read the stdlib the
manifest PINS. They compile with the compiler's lib/ overlay (`CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY`) set to a mirror of
`versions/<pin>/lib` in `dist/.dlverify-<pid>/`; the mirror leaves out what the project's own `./lib` holds (the
overlay outranks `./lib`, so a vendored leaf would otherwise be shadowed), has a `--locked` overlay laid over it, and
is removed afterwards. Through 6.7.13, under `CYRIUS_RESOLVED=1`, a leaf `./lib` lacked was read from the CLI's OWN
version slot — cycc resolves `lib/<leaf>.cyr` as overlay, CWD, the store slot of cycc's own version, and only then
`#@incdir`, so a mirror reached only through `#@incdir` was shadowed — and the self-check had no copy at all. The CLI
still strips an inherited `CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY` in `main()` (since 6.7.6), so only its own overlay reaches a compile.

[[entries]]
name = "fuzz_poison_allocator_seam_exit_86_and_ab_v6618"
description = "Since cyrius 6.6.18 (P6) `cyrius fuzz --poison` poisons `alloc()` (Linux / macOS / Windows), arenas, `fl_alloc` and anything routed through `poison_allocator()`: leading + trailing redzones, a live-block sweep, quarantine on free. The first overwrite prints one `poison: redzone overwrite — …` line and exits 86. Overreads are silent under plain `--poison`; `--poison=ab` runs every harness at fill 0xA5 and 0x5A and fails when the outputs differ"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["fuzz", "poison", "redzone", "allocator-seam", "poison_allocator", "exit-86", "ab-differential", "p6", "v6.6.18"]
---
== USE ==

    cyrius fuzz --poison            # writes trap (exit 86); assert_summary() sweeps too
    cyrius fuzz --poison=ab         # + overreads: two legs, byte-identical stdout required

`cyrius fuzz --poison` hands the compiler `CYRIUS_POISON=1` ahead of the shell's value; setting the
variable by hand takes `0` / `1` only since 6.7.11 (`0` and an empty value were ON before; a word is
refused by name — compiler_env_vars).

Route your own allocation through it:

    sd_alloc_set(&poison_alloc);              # a hook-style fn(n): ptr seam
    var v = vec_new_a(poison_allocator());    # any _a API
    var a = poison_allocator_over(inner);     # wrap an existing Allocator

`poison_sweep()` checks every live block now and returns the bad count; `poison_trap_set(0)` keeps
going (then assert on `poison_violations()`); `poison_fill_set(b)` takes 1..255; `poison_reset()`
resets the COUNTER only; `poison_forget_all()` drops the live list (call it after `alloc_reset()`
when using the seam without the flag).

⚠ Under poison an arena never re-issues an address after `arena_reset` — a fuzz harness must not
assert pointer identity across a reset. `--poison=ab` doubles the time, and a harness that prints
timings or seeds from the clock diverges by itself.

NOT covered: a read that jumps a whole redzone into a live neighbour, a read past a logical bound
inside one allocation, stack and static memory, agnos and cx `alloc()`; no guard pages (backlog).
Before 6.6.18 `--poison` reached `fl_alloc` only and COUNTED violations nobody read (0 of 194
ecosystem harnesses). CHANGELOG [6.6.18] (Poison — P6); the cyrius guide's "Fuzzing with --poison".

[[entries]]
name = "embed_data_files_as_name_fns_v6619"
description = "Since cyrius 6.6.19 (P2) `[embed] NAME = \"path\"` in cyrius.cyml gives every compile the file's bytes as `NAME()` (a NUL-terminated string) and `NAME_len()`, read at build time — no generated .cyr to check in, nothing to drift. `[lib] embed = [\"NAME\"]` / `[lib.P] embed` carry named embeds into a distlib bundle, and `distlib --check` goes STALE when the data file changes. Every bad entry is refused by name, exit 1"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["embed", "manifest", "cyrius.cyml", "distlib", "include_str", "data-files", "refusals", "p2", "v6.6.19"]
---
== USE ==

    [embed]
    PRESET_LEAN = "src/presets/lean.json"
    FACE        = "fonts/face.ttf"

    var p = PRESET_LEAN();         # a fn, NOT a var: a top-level string var is a deferred
    var n = PRESET_LEAN_len();     # runtime store, which a `kernel;` build runs after its program

    [lib.face]
    embed = ["FACE"]               # an embed-only profile is legal: a bundle of the bytes

cbt generates `fn NAME(): i64 { return "<escaped>"; }` + `fn NAME_len()` ahead of `#@srcline`, so
diagnostics keep their `<source>` line numbers. Bytes 0x20–0x7E stay raw except `"` / `\`; every
other byte (NULs included) is `\xHH`. `--print-config` lists the entries. A bundle carries ONLY the
embeds its `embed` key names, and is verified alone (no `[embed]` prelude, no `[build] modules`).

REFUSED, by name: absolute paths, `..`, `\`, `:` anywhere (NTFS streams), control bytes; any symlink,
junction or hard link (`st_nlink > 1`) on the path; any `.git` component and its aliases (`.git.`,
`GIT~1`, any `~`+digit 8.3 name, HFS+-ignorable code points); a missing / non-regular file; one file
over 2,097,150 B or a set over cycc's 2 MiB string pool; a NAME that is malformed, reserved (119
names, incl. `sizeof` / `mulh64` / `fncall0..8`), duplicated, `X` beside `X_len`, or ALREADY
DECLARED by the stdlib snapshot or the project (a silent "last definition wins" otherwise); the
`{dir, glob}` / inline-table / array set forms (explicit entries only, so order is yours).

LIMITS: the 2 MiB pool is shared with the program's own literals; every binary of the project
(every test binary too) carries every declared embed, even unused, even under DCE. (This line also said
"cyrius-lsp does not read the manifest, so `NAME()` shows undefined there" until 6.7.16 — false: the
server's project check, `cyrius check --with-deps`, prepends the [embed] modules as every compile does;
TL-14, measured.) Since 6.7.12 (WN-3) the open refuses a
link on EVERY target: Linux and both macOS walk the path from a handle on the project root (`openat`
with `O_DIRECTORY | O_NOFOLLOW` per component — arm64 macOS's `openat` keeps its dirfd since 6.7.12);
Windows, which has no such walk, opens the path and requires the OPENED handle's final path
(`GetFinalPathNameByHandleW`, `win_handle_under`) to lie inside the project root's, so a junction
planted after the check is refused ("cannot be opened through a link-free path"). `${file:}` takes
the same open. CHANGELOG [6.6.19] (Embed — P2), [6.7.12] WN-3; the cyrius guide's "Embedding data
files: [embed]".

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_cyml_git_first_local_switch_v676"
description = "Since cyrius 6.7.6 the committed cyrius.cyml means the TAGS: a `[deps.X]` with git + tag resolves the tag (commit pin checked) on every machine, and a `path` beside it is a DEV OVERRIDE read only in local mode (`CYRIUS_LOCAL=1` / `CYRIUS_LOCAL=a,b` / `--local`), which builds from build/local-deps/ and writes neither lib/ nor cyrius.lock. `--locked` / `CYRIUS_LOCKED=1` resolves and writes nothing; `deps -v` / `--dry-run` say where each dependency came from; a diamond and unknown keys are named; `cyrius update` re-fetches untagged deps. A dev- and test-friendliness change, not a security item."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrius.cyml", "deps", "git", "path", "local", "CYRIUS_LOCAL", "--locked", "CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY", "cyrius.lock", "update", "v6.7.6"]
---
== THE RULE (user, 2026-10-08) ==

The manifest syntax is unchanged; what a `path` beside `git` / `tag` MEANS changed:

    [deps.sigil]
    git  = "https://github.com/MacCracken/sigil"
    tag  = "3.9.9"
    path = "../sigil"          # dev override: read ONLY in local mode
    modules = ["dist/sigil.cyr"]

With no switch — CI and a developer's box alike — every machine builds the TAG, its commit pin checked,
and `lib/` + `cyrius.lock` record that resolution. So a local build and CI compile the same code. The
manifest can NEVER switch local mode on, so no commit flips what CI builds.

A `path` is relative to the MANIFEST's directory; an absolute one is taken as given. On Windows that
includes `C:/…`, `C:\…`, a rooted `\…`, a UNC path and a drive-relative `C:x` — cyrius.exe joined a
drive-rooted path onto the manifest's directory through 6.7.12 (6.7.13, TL-1).

== THE SWITCH ==

    export CYRIUS_LOCAL=1                   # every declared override (once, in a shell profile)
    CYRIUS_LOCAL=sigil,libro cyrius build   # only these names
    cyrius build --local                    # the flag form, any resolving verb; --local=sigil,libro
    cyrius build --no-local                 # the tags for this run, whatever CYRIUS_LOCAL says

Precedence: `--local[=..]` / `--no-local` > `CYRIUS_LOCAL` (`1`, `0` or a comma list) > off.
`cyrius build --print-config` shows it as `deps.local` (and `deps.locked`).

== WHAT A RUN SAYS ==

  - Default, a declared checkout exists but was not used — ONE line:
    `hint: 1 dep has a local checkout not in use (sigil); CYRIUS_LOCAL=1 builds it (this run built the tag)`
    — only where the switch works: never under `--locked` or a release verb.
  - Local mode, one line per override in use:
    `local: sigil <- ../sigil @1a2b3c4, 118 commits past 3.9.9, dirty — CI builds 3.9.9`
    A selected `path` that is not a directory is named and the tag builds instead; a selected NAME
    no manifest gives a `path` is warned (a typo must not read as "no override").

== LOCAL MODE WRITES NO TRACKED FILE ==

The whole resolution goes to `build/local-deps/lib/`; `lib/` and `cyrius.lock` stay what the tag
resolution wrote, so switching modes churns nothing (keep `build/` in .gitignore — local mode warns
when it is not). Include strings stay `lib/<file>`: cyrius sets `CYRIUS_LIB_OVERLAY=build/local-deps/lib`
for its compiles and cycc tries `<overlay>/<rest>` FIRST for an `include "lib/<rest>"`. Why an overlay:
include-once is keyed on the path STRING (the whole spelling, at any length, since 6.7.15 — IT-5; through
6.7.14 a spelling of 128+ characters was kept to 127 bytes, never matched, and was included again), so
prepending `build/local-deps/lib/sigil.cyr` beside a
source's own `include "lib/sigil.cyr"` compiled BOTH copies and the tag's won (last definition wins);
11 of the 21 consumers with a path override hand-include it. Never set the variable by hand (an
inherited value is removed); cycc ignores any value that is absolute, drive-qualified, holds `..` or
is over 480 bytes. Local mode with no override actually in use resolves exactly as the default.

== DEPENDENCIES OF DEPENDENCIES ==

  - A dependency resolved from its TAG has its own `path` entries IGNORED (its developers' override,
    not its published meaning), and a path-only, local-`git` (`git = "../x"`) or absolute entry in it
    is REFUSED by name: `error: mid's manifest names [deps.leaf] path = "../leaf" with no git / tag —
    a published dependency must name git + tag; refused`. It used to resolve on its author's box and
    fail in CI.
  - A dependency that is ITSELF a local checkout has its overrides followed for the SELECTED names, so
    a sibling stack builds from working trees: `local: leaf <- ../leaf (via mid) ...`.
  - A path-only dependency in the root manifest (no `git`) works as before, in every mode.

== RELEASE VERBS ==

`--locked`, `publish`, `package`, `distlib` and `update` resolve what a release ships: the switch is off
there, `--local` is refused by name, and a set `CYRIUS_LOCAL` gets one `note:` line.

== --locked / CYRIUS_LOCKED=1 — THE CI CHECK ==

Resolves the tags into a private scratch, verifies every commit pin against its clone, compares the
would-be `lib/` and `cyrius.lock` with the committed ones, WRITES NOTHING, and fails naming each
difference (stale lib/ bytes, a missing commit pin, a lock hash the tag disagrees with, an unlocked
lib/ file, a lock line for a file that exists nowhere, another stdlib pin); no `cyrius.lock` at all is
refused. Clean: `--locked: lib/ and cyrius.lock are exactly what the tags resolve`.
With no vendored `lib/` (a project that ignores it) the resolve is checked against `cyrius.lock` ALONE
(one `note:` line says so), and a compiling verb (`build --locked`, `test --locked`) compiles that
proven resolution from `build/locked-deps/lib/` through the same overlay. Locked means nothing is
written in ANY verb: under `CYRIUS_LOCKED=1`, `cyrius update`, `deps --lock`, `deps --relock` and
`lib sync` are refused by name; `deps --verify` and `lib sync --dry-run` still run. A path-only dep
is refused (a release cannot resolve a path). `--locked` does not combine with `--local`, `--relock`,
`--verify` or `--lock`. It REPLACES the hand-rolled CI guards — `git diff --exit-code -- cyrius.lock`,
`lock-check.sh`, `verify-lock.sh`, `cyrius deps && cyrius deps --verify` (whose first step rewrote the
lock the second checked) and the path-stripping `sed`.

== WHERE EACH DEPENDENCY CAME FROM ==

`cyrius deps -v` (any resolving verb with `-v`) ends with one line per dependency; `cyrius deps
--dry-run` prints the same WITHOUT resolving — no clone, no lib/, no lock — and fails where the real
run would:

    dependency sources:
      sigil  tag 3.9.9 @1a2b3c4 (cache)
      libro  local ../libro @9f8e7d6, 3 commits past 2.1.0
      leaf   tag 1.0 @5e6f7a8 (fetched) via mid

Test-scope dependencies are marked `[test scope]` (see test_scope_and_one_test_verb_v676).

== DIAMOND, UNKNOWN KEYS, UPDATE, OPERANDS ==

  - A diamond (one dependency wanted at two tags) still resolves closest-first and now says so:
    `note: leaf 2.0 (wanted by p2) not used; 1.0 (p1) resolved first`. Only an entry the run would
    resolve counts (an `optional` dep with no active feature, another `target`, is not named).
  - A key nothing reads in your own `[deps]` / `[deps.NAME]`, and a table nothing reads
    (`[dev-dependencies]`, `[[bin]]`), is warned by name, as `[build]`'s unknown keys are.
  - `cyrius update` writes what `cyrius lib sync` writes — the declared `[deps] stdlib` set (per-OS
    peers and the test scope's leaves) from the snapshot of `[package].cyrius`, with lib sync's lock
    guard and refusals; `--full` for the whole snapshot (D18, 6.7.12 — TL-16; it "refreshed lib/ from
    the toolchain" before). It needs that pin installed; it never reads `current`. It also
    RE-FETCHES each untagged dependency (it floats
    by design; the cache froze it at its first fetch), re-resolves, and lists newer version tags
    newest first — `sigil: 3.9.9 pinned; newer tags: 3.10.0, 3.9.10 (cyrius.cyml is not edited)`. It
    never edits the manifest: moving a tag is a decision made in the file.
  - Every resolving verb checks its operands BEFORE the resolve (`cyrius test <missing>` used to clone
    and lock first).

ADOPTION rides the pin: a repo pinned below 6.7.6 keeps the old resolution (a present `path` wins)
until it bumps `[package].cyrius`; then the commented-out `# path =` lines, "never commit path"
policies and CI guards against a committed path are no longer needed. NOT a security item (the user:
a dependency you chose already runs its code in your build).
GATES: tests/gates/toolchain/deps_git_first_local_switch.sh, deps_locked_writes_nothing.sh,
deps_sources_reported.sh, deps_update_refetches_untagged.sh, lib_overlay_include.sh,
manifest_unknown_keys_warned.sh, operands_checked_before_resolve.sh. CHANGELOG [6.7.6]; the cyrius
guide's "Build Tool & Dependencies".

[[entries]]
name = "test_scope_and_one_test_verb_v676"
description = "Since cyrius 6.7.6 test-only configuration has its own scope: a `[test]` section (files, stdlib, modules, defines, timeout, [test.embed]), `[deps.X] scope = \"test\"` and a per-directory test.cyml reach only test / bench / fuzz compiles; each unit gets CYRIUS_TEST_FILE / CYRIUS_TEST_DIR; `--timeout`; the exit code is the contract. `cyrius test <file|dir>...` absorbs `cyrius tests` (a deprecated alias for one release), and `[build] test_standalone = true` compiles each test with its own includes only."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrius test", "cyrius tests", "[test]", "test.cyml", "scope", "test_standalone", "CYRIUS_TEST_FILE", "timeout", "exit code", "v6.7.6"]
---
== THE [test] SECTION — the test scope only ==

    [test]
    files   = ["src/test.cyr"]                 # what a bare `cyrius test` runs first, then tests/
    stdlib  = ["assert", "bench"]              # stdlib leaves for the test scope only
    modules = ["tests/support/fixtures.cyr"]   # prepended after [build] modules
    defines = ["TESTING"]                      # one #define NAME each
    timeout = 300                              # seconds per unit; 0 = no deadline

    [test.embed]
    GOLDEN = "tests/data/golden.bin"           # the [embed] rules

    [deps.mockpeer]
    git     = "https://github.com/MacCracken/mockpeer"
    tag     = "0.3.1"
    modules = ["dist/mockpeer.cyr"]
    scope   = "test"                           # resolved + pinned like any dep; test scope only

The TEST SCOPE is the compiles of `cyrius test`, `bench` and `fuzz` (and `cyrius audit`'s test / bench
sweep); `build`, `run`, `distlib` and `package` never see it. Without the section nothing changes.
  - `files`: `[build] test` is its older spelling (41 manifests), read when `[test] files` is absent;
    with both, `[test] files` is read and `[build] test` is warned by name.
  - `stdlib` leaves are vendored and locked by EVERY resolve, whichever verb ran it (otherwise build
    and test take turns rewriting the lock), and only the test scope PREPENDS them. A production
    binary stops carrying assert / bench (a hello world: 111,456 B with them in `[deps] stdlib`,
    98,280 B with them here) and a `dist/<pkg>.deps` sidecar never names them. `cyrius init` writes
    assert / bench here.
  - `modules` follow `[build] modules`; one that cannot be read fails the unit by name.
  - `defines`: `-D` > `CYRIUS_DEFINES` > `[test] defines`, each REPLACING the list (CYRIUS_DEFINES
    reaches test and bench compiles since 6.7.6); fuzz harnesses also get `[build] defines` first.
  - `timeout`: `--timeout N` > `CYRIUS_TEST_TIMEOUT` > `[test] timeout` > 300 s; 0 = none. A unit past
    it is killed with its whole process tree and named.
  - `[test.embed]` NAMEs must differ from `[embed]`'s; a clash names the table that has the NAME.
  - `scope = "test"` is the only scope value (anything else refused). The dep resolves AFTER every
    production dep, so a name both sides reach is the production one (the diamond notice names the
    test-scope tag not used); a dependency's own test-scope entries are never walked by a consumer;
    `deps -v` / `--dry-run` mark `[test scope]`; `--locked` covers it.

== test.cyml — A DIRECTORY'S OWN [test] ==

    # tests/net/test.cyml
    [test]
    stdlib  = ["net"]
    defines = ["NET_TESTS"]
    timeout = 60
    [test.embed]
    CAPTURE = "capture.bin"        # relative to this directory

Applies to every test-scope unit under its directory. Each level APPENDS (`cyrius.cyml`'s `[test]`,
then `./test.cyml`, `tests/test.cyml`, `tests/net/test.cyml`); an inner `timeout` REPLACES. It carries
NO pin and NO dependencies: `[package]`, `[deps]`, `[build]`, any other table and `[test] files` are
refused by name — nothing in it can drift from the project (the job per-test sub-manifests with their
own pins used to do). Every resolve vendors the stdlib leaves of the test.cyml beside cyrius.cyml and
of every one under tests/, benches/, fuzz/. However a unit is spelt (`./tests/./net/x.tcyr`, absolute,
`tests\net\x.tcyr` on Windows) it reads one chain, each level once; a unit outside the project gets
`cyrius.cyml`'s `[test]` only.

== AT RUN TIME ==

Each unit gets `CYRIUS_TEST_FILE` (the unit) and `CYRIUS_TEST_DIR` (its directory) in its environment —
absolute where the host can say so (relative on Windows today, backlog) — so a test finds data beside
itself whatever the cwd. Compile-time fixtures are `[test] modules` and `[test.embed]`.

== ONE TEST VERB ==

    cyrius test                          # [test] files first, then every .tcyr under tests/ — each once
    cyrius test tests/net/x.tcyr         # one file
    cyrius test tests/tcyr/crossos       # a directory: every .tcyr under it, RECURSIVE
    cyrius test a.tcyr tests/net         # several operands, files and directories; each file once
    cyrius tests tests/net               # 6.7.6 – 6.7.11 a DEPRECATED alias; REMOVED at 6.7.12 (D21):
      Unknown command: tests
      error: `cyrius tests` was removed at 6.7.12: use `cyrius test <dir>` (`cyrius test tests` for tests/)

A directory holding no `.tcyr` is a named failure (exit 1). A bare `cyrius tests` was `cyrius test
tests` (it walked tests/ and never read `[test] files`); since 6.7.12 every `cyrius tests` form is
refused (rc 1) before any parse, resolve or run — a CI line calling it fails (TL-7). Until 6.7.6 `cyrius test <dir>` was `error: not a file` and the walk was the second verb —
the "test takes a FILE, tests takes a DIRECTORY" warning CLAUDE.md carried is retired.

`[build] test_standalone = true` compiles each test (every form, and `cyrius audit`'s test sweep) with
ONLY the includes the file writes — no `[deps] stdlib`, `[test] stdlib` or `scope = "test"` prepend;
`[embed]`, modules and defines still apply (`--no-deps` does the same for one run). For self-contained
suites: the cyrius repo sets it because 22 of its `.tcyr` define `fn run()`, a duplicate of
`lib/process.cyr`'s `run(cmd, arg1, arg2)` under the stdlib prepend.

== THE EXIT CODE IS THE CONTRACT (gated) ==

`cyrius test` — bare, a file, a directory, several operands — exits non-zero on any failing test (an
assertion, a signal, a timeout, an assert summary reporting a failure), any compile failure (a test, a
refused `[test]` value, the dependency resolve) and when it finds no tests; 0 exactly when every test
it found passed. A CI step is one line under `set -e` — no loop over files, no grep of the summary.
A missing operand (any of several) is refused by name BEFORE the resolve: no clone, no lib/, no lock.
The FAIL line says which: `(exit 3)`, `(killed by signal 11 SIGSEGV — ...)`, `(timed out after ...)`;
the signal is read from the wait status, so a test that EXITS 232 says `(exit 232)` (it used to say
"killed by signal 104"). `test`, `run`, `bench` and `fuzz` take `--no-deps`, `--no-lock`, `--locked`,
`--local`; `test`, `bench`, `fuzz` take `--timeout N`.
GATES: tests/gates/toolchain/test_section.sh, deps_test_scope.sh, test_cyml_per_directory.sh,
test_absorbs_tests.sh, test_exit_code_contract.sh. CHANGELOG [6.7.6]; the cyrius guide's "Tests:
[test], scope = test, test.cyml".

[[entries]]
name = "execution_coverage_mode_v6710"
description = "Since cyrius 6.7.10 `cyrius coverage --exec[=<glob>]` / `[coverage] exec = [\"programs/*_test.cyr\"]` measures what RUNS: every tests/**/*.tcyr and every named program is built with CYRIUS_COVERAGE=1 (one hit counter per fn, appended to CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE when the program exits), run, and its entered fns merged into the report (`Functions executed: E/T`); a nonzero exit, a signal, a timeout or a run with no coverage record fails the run by name. [coverage] programs stays TEXT coverage"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["coverage", "execution-coverage", "CYRIUS_COVERAGE", "cyrius.cyml", "P5", "v6.7.10"]
---
    [coverage]
    exec = ["programs/*_test.cyr"]            # alone turns the mode on

    cyrius coverage --exec                     # .tcyr + [coverage] exec, built instrumented and run
    cyrius coverage --exec='bench/*.cyr' -v    # replaces the list; -v lists the unexecuted fns
    cyrius coverage --exec --min 80            # --min gates the EXECUTED %

    # the compiler switch itself (any compile):
    CYRIUS_COVERAGE=1 CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE=$PWD/build/x.cov cyrius build x.cyr build/x
    # build/x.cov after a run:
    #   # cyrius-coverage 1
    #   3 <source>:1 sq                      <- <count> <file>:<line> <name>
    #   - <source>:4 nk                      <- a #naked fn: listed, not counted
    #   3 <source>:6 __cl00008               <- a closure (its number is an index; it moves)

- per fn, not per line or branch: the counter is bumped at the first byte of the body (after the
  frame, the parameters and the by-value struct copies). A fn never entered has no line. Counted:
  fns, methods, generic instances (`gen$i32`, `gen$f64` — a bare `gen$i` through 6.7.10 —, `gen$.f64` for a
  struct named like a scalar; an i64 instance is `gen`), `#derive`d fns (named at the
  struct's closing `}`), closures, an `async fn`'s body (`f$impl`, once per future), each `#inline`
  replay, every self tail call. Not counted: the async / enum-variant constructors, `__cyrius_*`,
  `#naked` fns (listed with `-`);
- the CLI counts by NAME (a record's name field, whole, cut at the first `$`; `<source>` = the entry's
  own fns, `__cyrius_*` and closures skipped) — the path holds identifiers (`src/shapes.cyr` →
  `shapes`), and so does a mangled suffix (`gen$f64`);
- dumps at the top level's end / `main`'s return and before every exit syscall whose NUMBER is a
  compile-time constant (a literal, a `const`, an enum value — `sys_exit`, `panic`, the stdlib's aborts,
  a bounds violation's 134), after the syscall's arguments are evaluated (`syscall(60, f())` counts f).
  Exit numbers per target: x86 / x86 macOS / cx 60 + 231, aarch64 / arm64 macOS 60 + 93 + 94, Windows
  60, agnos 0;
- NOT seen: an `asm` exit, a run-time exit number, an exit inside `#naked`, `execve`, a signal / crash /
  kill — the CLI names such a run ("exited 0 but left no coverage record") instead of reading 0;
- CYRIUS_COVERAGE_FILE is required, no default (a predictable name in a shared dir is
  symlink-plantable); the path is fixed at compile time and opened at exit, relative to the cwd THEN —
  give an absolute one; created 0644, opened O_APPEND (one block per process and run); a dump that
  cannot open it writes nothing and says nothing;
- one block per process: a forked child's block repeats the pre-fork counts; threads share the counters
  (non-atomic: a count can be lost, an entered fn never reads 0); only hit-ness is stable, and that is all
  the CLI reads;
- refused for kernel / EFI / `shared;` / `object;`; combines with the bounds-checked mode; every hosted
  target (x86-64 / aarch64 Linux, arm64 / x86-64 macOS, Windows, agnos, cx). cx auto-calls `main` since 6.7.11
  (as every image target does) and its dump counts it; the CLI never runs cx;
- reserved: `__cyrius_cov_cp`, `__cyrius_cov_num`, `__cyrius_cov_w`, `__cyrius_cov_dump` (a user fn
  reusing a `__cyrius_cov_` name is replaced, with the `duplicate fn` warning);
- a program built without the mode carries not a byte of it. Cost: ~30 B of code per fn body on x86-64
  + 8 B counter + a map line; an instrumented cycc +13 % size, +12 % self-compile (measured at 6.7.10).

GATES: tests/gates/codegen/coverage_exec_hits.sh, tests/gates/toolchain/coverage_exec_cli.sh,
tests/gates/memory/coverage_mode_corpus.sh. The guide: *The execution-coverage mode (6.7.10)*.
CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (P5-B; proposal P5 archived).
